codes.explanation:
You used something other than a whole number to index an array

Array indices in Turf have to be plain integers -- things like 0, 1, 2, and so on. If you try to use a decimal number, a string, a bool, or anything else as the position inside the square brackets, the compiler stops you here.

    immut primes: int[3] = [2, 3, 5];
    mut i: int = 1;
    mut val: int = primes[i];      // correct -- 'i' is a whole number

If you use something that isn't a whole number:

    mut d: double = 1.0;
    mut val: int = primes[d];      // 'd' is a double, not an int

Even though `1.0` looks like it obviously means index 1, Turf won't guess for you -- it wants you to be explicit, since not every double cleanly represents a whole number (what would `primes[1.5]` even mean?).

If the value you have really is meant to be a whole number, convert it first with `to int`:

    mut val: int = primes[d to int];

A `char` is not allowed as an index either -- a character is a letter, not a position. If you want its character code (say, to count how often each letter appears), convert it explicitly:

    mut code: int = (word[i] to int);
    counts[code] = counts[code] + 1;

Once the value you're indexing with is a genuine `int`, this error goes away. This one usually shows up early on, right around when you're first mixing doubles and array indices together -- a quick `to int` sorts it out.

You tried to for-each over something that isn't an array

Turf's `for value in something { ... }` loop only knows how to walk over one kind of thing: a real, built-in array (`int[3]`, `string[10]`, and so on). If `something` is a plain number, a struct, a `List<T>`, or anything else, the compiler stops you here instead of trying to guess what you meant.

    mut nums: int[3] = [1, 2, 3];
    for value in nums {
        io::printline(value to string);
    }

If you try it on something that isn't an array:

    mut n: int = 5;
    for value in n {
        io::printline(value to string);
    }

`n` is just a single number -- there's nothing to loop "over". Turf tells you exactly what type it found where an array was expected.

If what you actually have is a `List<T>` (or another collection type) rather than a fixed-size array, for-each doesn't support it directly yet -- drive a regular counting loop off the list's own `.length()` instead, and access elements through the list's own methods:

    for i in 0..my_list.length() step i += 1 {
        // work with my_list here using its own methods
    }

Once the thing after `in` is a genuine array, the for-each loop works exactly as expected. This is a quick fix once you know the rule: for-each is specifically for Turf's fixed-size arrays.

E006 is retired -- for-each over an unsized array parameter works now

Older versions of Turf refused `for value in arr { ... }` when `arr` was a parameter written `arr: int[]` (no size in the brackets), because such a parameter didn't carry its length with it. It does now: a `T[]` parameter knows how many elements the caller's array has, so this compiles and loops over every one of them.

    fn sum(arr: int[]): int {
        mut total: int = 0;
        for value in arr {          // fine -- visits every element
            total = total + value;
        }
        return total;
    }

    fn main(): int {
        mut a: int[3] = [1, 2, 3];
        mut b: int[5] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
        sum(a);                     // 6
        sum(b);                     // 15
        return 0;
    }

`arr.length()` gives the same count, and `arr[i]` is checked against it. The number E006 is kept so an old log that mentions it still has somewhere to land, and is never reused.

If you see an error about an unsized array today, it's E140: `T[]` written somewhere other than a function parameter. Run `turf --explain E140`.

You used break, continue, or return somewhere it doesn't belong

`break` and `continue` only make sense inside a loop -- they're instructions for "stop this loop right now" or "skip to the next round of this loop." If the compiler finds one sitting outside any `while` or `for`, there's nothing for it to break out of or continue, so it stops you here.

Here's `break` and `continue` used the right way, inside a `for` loop:

    fn sum_positive(nums: int[5]): int {
        mut total: int = 0;
        for i in 0..5 {
            if nums[i] < 0 {
                continue;
            }
            if total > 1000 {
                break;
            }
            total = total + nums[i];
        }
        return total;
    }

And here's the mistake -- `break` sitting inside an `if`, with no loop around it at all:

    fn broken(): void {
        if true {
            break;   // there's no loop here for this to break out of
        }
    }

A plain `if`/`else` block does NOT count as a loop, even though it also uses `{ }`. Only `while` and `for` create a loop that `break`/`continue` can target.

If you meant to write a loop, wrap the code in a `while` or `for`. If the `break`/`continue` was left over from some earlier version of the code, it's safe to just delete it.

This one's usually a quick fix once you spot which keyword is in the wrong spot -- happens to everyone while you're still getting a feel for where loops start and end.

You're reading a variable that might not have a value yet

In Turf, you're allowed to declare a variable without giving it a starting value right away -- `mut x: int;` is totally fine on its own. But before you actually read that variable, every possible path your program could take needs to have given it a real value first. If there's even one path where that never happens, Turf stops you before it becomes a bug.

The classic way to trip this is an if without a matching else:

    mut x: int;
    if some_condition {
        x = 1;
    }
    mut y: int = x;   // what if some_condition was false? x was never set!

Turf can't know at compile time whether `some_condition` will be true or false when your program actually runs, so it has to assume both are possible -- and on the "false" path, `x` never gets a value. Reading it afterward would read garbage, so Turf catches it now instead of letting that happen.

The fix is usually one of two things. Either give the variable a starting value right when you declare it:

    mut x: int = 0;

...or make sure every branch assigns it, by adding the missing else:

    mut x: int;
    if some_condition {
        x = 1;
    } else {
        x = 2;
    }
    mut y: int = x;   // now every path sets x before this line

A fixed-size array such as `mut a: int[5];` is different: it has a value from its declaration, with every element zero until you write it, so reading it is never this error.

Either approach works -- pick whichever makes more sense for what your program is actually trying to do. Once every path is guaranteed to set the variable before it's read, this error goes away and you're good to go.

Your function promises a value back, but doesn't always give one

When you write `fn abs(x: int): int`, you're promising that every single time this function finishes, it hands back an `int`. Turf checks every possible path through your function's body to make sure that promise actually holds -- and it found at least one path that reaches the end of the function without ever hitting a `return`.

Here's a version that covers every path:

    fn abs(x: int): int {
        if x < 0 {
            return -x;
        }
        return x;
    }

And here's the same function missing its safety net:

    fn abs(x: int): int {
        if x < 0 {
            return -x;
        }
    }

It looks complete at a glance, but think it through: if `x` is 0 or positive, the `if` never runs, and the function just... ends, with nothing to give back. That's exactly the gap Turf is pointing out.

The fix is almost always one of two things: add a final `return` after the `if` to catch every path that isn't covered yet, or add an `else` branch that also returns something. Either way, walk through your function's logic and ask "what happens if none of my `if`s are true?" -- that's usually where the missing return needs to go.

This is one of the most common things to run into as you're learning -- it just means the compiler is making sure your function always keeps its promise, which is a genuinely good thing to have checked for you automatically.

Your recursive function never stops calling itself

A recursive function is one that calls itself to solve a smaller version of the same problem -- like `factorial(n)` calling `factorial(n - 1)`. For that to ever finish, there needs to be a "stop here" case: some condition where the function just returns an answer instead of calling itself again. Turf looked at this function and couldn't find one -- every path through it calls itself again, no matter what.

Here's a `factorial` function with a proper stopping point:

    fn factorial(n: int): int {
        if n <= 1 {
            return 1;
        }
        return n * factorial(n - 1);
    }

And here's the same function without one:

    fn factorial(n: int): int {
        return n * factorial(n - 1);
    }

Run this in your head: `factorial(3)` calls `factorial(2)`, which calls `factorial(1)`, which calls `factorial(0)`, which calls `factorial(-1)`... and it just keeps going forever, calling itself with smaller and smaller numbers with nothing to ever stop it. In a real program this crashes once it runs out of stack space to keep track of all those calls.

The fix is to add an `if` (or similar check) that returns a plain answer once you've reached the simplest version of the problem -- for `factorial`, that's `n <= 1` returning `1`. Then double check that each recursive call is actually getting closer to that stopping point (here, `n - 1` moves toward `n <= 1` one step at a time).

Recursion can feel tricky the first few times -- the trick that helps most people is to always write the "stop here" case first, before you write the part that calls itself again.

Something about this code breaks one of Turf's basic rules

This error covers a handful of specific rules that don't really belong under any of Turf's other, more specific errors. The exact message tells you which rule -- here are the ones you're most likely to actually run into:

You can't call `main` yourself:

    fn main(): int {
        return 0;
    }

    main();   // not allowed -- main is your program's starting point, not a function you call

`main` runs automatically when your program starts. Calling it again from inside your own code doesn't make sense, so Turf stops you.

You also can't give `main` its own parameters:

    fn main(x: int): int {   // not allowed
        return 0;
    }

`main` always has the same fixed shape -- no parameters, just `fn main(): int`. If you need input from outside your program, that comes through other means, not through `main`'s own parameter list.

If you're using a `compare`/`matches` block, every pattern you match against has to be something Turf can figure out at compile time, like a plain number -- not something computed while the program runs.

If you happen to see a message that starts with "Internal error", that one's different -- it means the compiler itself hit a situation it wasn't expecting, which is a bug in Turf, not a mistake in your code. It's worth reporting rather than trying to work around.

For everything else under this error, the message itself tells you exactly which rule got broken -- just follow what it says and you'll be past it in no time.

You wrote code that comes right after a return, so it can never run

Once a function hits `return`, it's done -- control leaves right there and nothing else in that same block gets a chance to execute. Turf noticed a statement sitting right after a `return` (or `break`/`continue`) in the same block, which means that statement is dead code: it's there in your file, but the program can never actually reach it.

Here's the mistake:

    fn example(): int {
        return 5;
        io::printline("this never prints");
    }

That `printline` call looks perfectly normal, but the function already returned on the line above it, so it will never run -- not once, no matter what.

If that statement wasn't meant to run at all, just delete it. If you actually wanted it to happen, move it to BEFORE the `return`:

    fn example(): int {
        io::printline("this prints now");
        return 5;
    }

This one's easy to run into when you're moving code around while editing a function -- a quick reread of the lines right after a `return`/`break`/`continue` usually makes it obvious which case you're in.

E013 is retired -- code after a return is now always E012

Older versions of Turf had two codes for the same mistake: E012 for a statement that comes after a `return`, `break` or `continue`, and E013 for a *declaration* in that position. In practice E012 always fired first, so E013 was documented and nobody ever saw it. The number is kept so an old log that mentions it still has somewhere to land, and is never reused.

    fn compute(): int {
        return 1;
        mut x: int = 5;        // can never run -- E012
    }

Run `turf --explain E012` for the explanation and the fix: delete the code, or move it before the `return`.

I don't recognize that word here

Turf expected a real keyword at this spot in your code -- something like `if`, `while`, `struct`, or `return` -- but the word it found isn't one of Turf's actual keywords. This is almost always a small typo.

    if x > 0 {
        return x;
    }

If you accidentally typed it wrong:

    iff x > 0 {   // typo! Turf doesn't know the word "iff"
        return x;
    }

The good news is Turf usually already knows what you meant and will suggest the real keyword right in the error message, so you can just fix the spelling and keep going.

If you're not sure what the actual keyword should be, it's worth glancing at a piece of working Turf code with a similar structure -- `if`/`elseif`/`else`, `while`, `for ... in ... step`, `struct`, `enum`, `fn`, `return`, `mut`/`immut`, and a handful of others are the reserved words Turf understands. Once the spelling matches one of those exactly, you're all set.

You're binding an error that has nothing worth inspecting

Turf's `recover as err run { ... }` exists so you can look at what actually went wrong -- `err` gives you access to the error's own data, so you can react differently depending on which kind of failure happened. But the function you called here can only ever fail with a "bare" error: one declared as just `error SomethingWentWrong;`, with no extra information attached at all. There's nothing on a bare error worth binding a name to and inspecting, so Turf stops you here instead of letting you write a check that can never actually tell you anything useful.

Here's a bare error and a call that fails with it:

    error GeneralError;

    fn maybe_fail(should_fail: bool): !int {
        if should_fail {
            return GeneralError::DEFAULT;
        }
        return 99;
    }

Since `GeneralError` is bare, you don't need (and can't use) `recover as err`:

    fn main(): int {
        return maybe_fail(true) recover produce -1;
    }

And here's the version that trips this error:

    fn main(): int {
        return maybe_fail(true) recover as err run {
            return -1;
        };
    }

The fix is simple: swap `recover as err run { ... }` for either `recover produce <value>` (just gives back a fallback value on failure) or `recover run { ... }` (runs some code on failure, without needing to inspect what went wrong). Save `recover as err` for functions that fail with a richer error type -- one that actually carries data worth looking at.

You freed the same memory twice

When you call `mem::free(buf)`, you're telling the computer "I'm done with this memory, it's safe to give back." Once that happens, the memory isn't yours to manage anymore -- so freeing the exact same pointer a second time is a real bug, not just a style issue. It confuses the system that keeps track of what memory is in use, which can cause strange crashes or corrupted data later in ways that are genuinely hard to track down. Turf catches this for you at compile time instead, before it ever becomes that kind of mess.

Here's the mistake:

    unsafe {
        mut buf: int* = mem::malloc(40);
        mem::free(buf);
        mem::free(buf);   // buf was already freed on the line above
    }

And here's the safe version -- free it once, and set it to `null` afterward so there's nothing left to accidentally free again:

    unsafe {
        mut buf: int* = mem::malloc(40);
        mem::free(buf);
        buf = null;
    }

If you're seeing this error, look for a code path where the same pointer might get freed more than once -- often this happens when a function has multiple `return` points and more than one of them tries to clean up the same allocation. Setting a pointer to `null` right after freeing it is a good habit in general, since it makes an accidental second free much easier to spot.

Manual memory management (the `unsafe`/`mem::malloc`/`mem::free` world) is genuinely one of the trickier things to get right in any language -- this error existing at all is Turf catching a mistake for you before it turns into a much harder bug to find later.

You used memory after already freeing it

`mem::free(buf)` tells the computer "I'm done with this memory, you can use it for something else now." Once that's happened, reading or writing through `buf` again is genuinely risky -- that memory might still look fine for a while, or it might already contain something completely different, and there's no reliable way to tell which. Turf catches this for you at compile time so you never end up debugging a mysterious runtime crash caused by reading memory that isn't yours anymore.

Here's the mistake:

    unsafe {
        mut buf: int* = mem::malloc(40);
        mem::free(buf);
        unsafe { buf[0] = 5; }   // buf was already freed above
    }

And here's the safe order -- use the memory first, free it last:

    unsafe {
        mut buf: int* = mem::malloc(40);
        unsafe { buf[0] = 5; }
        mem::free(buf);
    }

The fix is almost always about ordering: make sure every place you read from or write to a pointer happens BEFORE the `mem::free(...)` call for it, not after. If your function has multiple exit points, double-check that none of them try to use a pointer that an earlier path already freed.

A helpful habit once you do free a pointer: set it to `null` right afterward. That way, if you (or future you) accidentally try to use it again, you get an obvious null-related error instead of a confusing one about stale memory.

Two of your files are waiting on each other to finish loading

Imagine module A saying "I need to import B before I can start," and module B saying "I need to import A before I can start." Neither one can ever actually finish -- that's a circular import, and Turf catches it instead of letting your program end up with mysterious missing functions.

    // a.tr
    import b;
    fn use_b(): void { b::do_something(); }

    // b.tr
    import a;
    fn do_something(): void { a::use_b(); }

Here, a.tr imports b.tr, and b.tr imports a.tr right back. Turf will print out the whole chain so you can see exactly which files are involved, even if the cycle is longer than two files.

This usually means two files each ended up needing something small from the other one. There are two good ways to fix it:

  - If one of the imports genuinely isn't needed, just remove it.
  - If both files really do need to share something, move that shared piece into a third file that both of them import -- so neither one has to import the other.

    // shared.tr
    fn do_something(): void { /* the shared logic */ }

    // a.tr
    import shared;

    // b.tr
    import shared;

Once nothing points back to where it started, the cycle is broken and both files can load normally. This is a very natural thing to run into once your project has more than a couple of files talking to each other -- it just means it's time to pull the shared piece out into its own place.

You declared the same extern function two different ways

`extern fn` is how Turf talks to real C functions, and it identifies them purely by name -- not by their full signature the way ordinary Turf functions are. That means if you (or two different files you both import) declare `extern fn` for the same name but describe it with different parameter types or a different return type, Turf can no longer tell which description is the real one.

    // strings.tr
    extern fn strlen(s: string): int32;

    // net.tr
    extern fn strlen(s: int32): int32;

Both of these claim to describe the C function `strlen`, but they disagree about what it takes as a parameter. Turf has no way to know which one is actually correct, so it stops and asks you to fix it rather than silently picking one and possibly miscompiling every call to the other.

Declaring the exact same `extern fn` more than once is completely fine, by the way -- it's common for two different library files to both need the same C function (like `strlen`) and both declare it. That's not an error, as long as every declaration matches word for word.

To fix this, make sure every `extern fn` for a given name has the exact same parameter types, return type, and does the same thing:

    // strings.tr
    extern fn strlen(s: string): int32;

    // net.tr
    extern fn strlen(s: string): int32;   // now they match

If you actually meant to call two genuinely different C functions, give one of them a different name on the Turf side instead of reusing the same one.

An exported constant needs a value Turf can know right away

`export immut` shares a constant with every file that imports it, but it does that by baking the literal value directly into each importing file at compile time -- there's no shared storage behind the scenes. That means the value has to be something Turf can figure out immediately, like a plain number (a negative one such as -1 too), string, or true/false, not something computed from another variable or a function call.

    immut base: int = 10;
    export immut total: int = base * 2;

Even though `base * 2` is obviously 20 to a person reading this, Turf's exported constants can only hold a literal value written directly, not an expression -- even a simple one like this. The fix is just to write out the actual value:

    export immut total: int = 20;

If the real value genuinely needs to be computed (not just a simple literal you can write by hand), it can't be an exported constant. Instead, keep it as a normal (non-exported) variable and compute it wherever it's actually used:

    immut base: int = 10;
    immut total: int = base * 2;   // fine -- not exported, so any expression works

This is a small tradeoff for how `export immut` works, but it's a fair one: it means every file that imports a constant gets a value with zero runtime cost, right there at compile time.

The same rule explains the other way to trip this error: leaving the value out altogether.

    export immut MAX_RETRIES: int;      // nothing to export

An exported constant *is* its value, so it has to be given one on the spot:

    export immut MAX_RETRIES: int = 3;

`export` only works at the top level of a file, not inside a function

The whole point of `export` is to share a constant with other files that import this one. That only makes sense for something declared at the top level of a file -- if you write `export` on a declaration inside a function body, it's contradicting itself: local to one function call, but also supposedly shared everywhere.

    fn setup(): void {
        export immut ready: bool = true;
    }

Here, `ready` is declared inside `setup()`, which means it doesn't even exist until `setup()` actually runs -- there's nothing for another file to import ahead of time. Turf catches this and asks you to pick one: either it lives at the top level and is genuinely shared, or it stays local and drops the `export`.

If you really do want other files to be able to use this value, move it out to the top level of the file:

    export immut ready: bool = true;

    fn setup(): void {
        // ready is available here too, and to any file that imports this one
    }

If it was only ever meant to be used inside that one function, it doesn't need `export` at all -- just declare it normally:

    fn setup(): void {
        immut local_flag: bool = true;
    }

Either way, the fix is quick once you decide which of those two things you actually meant.

Two different imports both want to give you something called the same thing

Turf lets you import a specific name straight out of a module with `import module::name;`, so you can call it without qualifying it every time. But if two different modules you import both happen to have something with the same bare name, Turf can't guess which one you mean when you just write the name by itself -- so it stops and asks you to be specific.

    import shapes::area;
    import geometry::area;

Both of these are real functions, each declared in its own module -- neither one is a mistake by itself. The problem is only that once they're both imported under the bare name `area`, Turf genuinely doesn't know which `area` you mean if you just write `area(...)`.

The easiest fix is to stop relying on the bare name and just qualify one (or both) calls with their module name instead:

    shapes::area(...);
    geometry::area(...);

If you only actually need one of the two, you can also narrow the second import so it doesn't bring in the conflicting name at all -- for example, if you imported everything from a module with a wildcard, switch to listing only the specific names you need:

    import geometry::[perimeter];   // no longer imports 'area', no more collision

Either way works fine -- pick whichever keeps your code clearest. This kind of naming clash is pretty rare in small projects, but it's a good thing Turf catches it the moment it happens instead of letting one function quietly shadow the other.

You tried to overload an operator for two types Turf already handles

Operator overloading lets you teach Turf what `+`, `-`, `*`, and friends should do for your OWN struct types -- like adding two `Point`s together. But Turf already knows how to add two `int`s, compare two `bool`s, and so on, so it won't let you redeclare an operator where BOTH sides are builtin types (`int`, `double`, `bool`, `char`, `string`, `int32`). If that were allowed, `1 + 2` would become ambiguous -- which version runs, the language's or yours?

Here's a valid overload, where at least one side is your own struct:

    struct Point {
        x: int,
        y: int
    }

    operator +(a: Point, b: Point): Point {
        mut r: Point = Point::default();
        r.x = a.x + b.x;
        r.y = a.y + b.y;
        return r;
    }

And here's what triggers this error -- both sides are builtin types:

    operator +(a: int, b: int): int {
        return a;
    }

To fix it, do one of two things:

  - If you meant to add behavior for your own struct, change one of the parameter types from a builtin to your struct's name -- `operator +(a: Point, b: int): Point` works fine, since one side is now a struct.
  - If you were just trying to use `+` on two plain numbers, you don't need an overload at all -- delete it and use `a + b` directly, it already does what you want.

This one's really just Turf protecting you from accidentally breaking how ordinary math works everywhere else in your program.

You used '::' on a variable, but '::' is only for types, enums, and modules

Turf splits access into two operators, and it's an easy thing to mix up while you're still getting used to it. `.` is for things you already HOLD -- an instance's fields and methods. `::` is for looking something up by NAME, without needing an instance at all -- constructing a struct, picking an enum variant, calling a module function, or calling a static method.

So this works, because `p` is a real `Point` you're holding, and `.` is instance access:

    mut p: Point = Point::new(1, 2);
    p.sum();

But this doesn't, because `p` is a variable holding an instance, and `::` isn't for that:

    p::sum();
    // ERROR: `p` is a variable, not a type or module, so I cannot use `::` on it.

Notice that `Point::new(1, 2)` on the first line is still correct -- that's `::` on the TYPE name `Point`, which is exactly what `::` is for. The only thing that changed on the second line is that `p` is now an instance, so it needs `.` instead.

The fix is simple: wherever you see this error, swap that one `::` for a `.`. The name after it (the method or field) is very likely already spelled right -- it's just the operator that needs to change.

You called something that doesn't exist inside this module

The module itself is fine -- you imported it correctly, and Turf found it without any trouble. The problem is more specific: you asked for a function or type inside that module that isn't actually there.

Here's a correct call into the `io` module:

    import io;
    io::printline("hello");

And here's the kind of mistake that trips this error -- a typo in the function name:

    import io;
    io::printlne("hello");
    // ERROR: I cannot find `printlne` in the module `io`.

Just like with struct fields, Turf will usually notice how close your typo is to a real name and suggest it for you, so you can fix the spelling and move on.

If it's not a typo, double-check two other things: that you're calling into the right module (it's easy to expect a function to live in one module when it's actually declared in another), and that the function or type wasn't renamed or removed since you last used it. Looking at the module's own source file is the most reliable way to see exactly what it exports.

This is a completely normal thing to hit while you're still learning what each module in the standard library actually offers -- nothing to stress about, just a quick spelling or module check away from working.

Turf doesn't recognize the name you put before '::'

`::` always needs something real on its left side -- an enum, a struct, or an imported module. This error means Turf looked at the name you wrote before `::` and couldn't match it to any of those three things at all. That's a slightly different problem than "the module exists but doesn't have that function" (which is a separate error) -- here, the very first name wasn't recognized.

Here's correct usage of all three kinds:

    import io;
    io::printline("hello");

    enum Color { Red, Green, Blue }
    immut c: Color = Color::Red;

    mut p: Point = Point::new(1, 2);

The most common way to trip this is forgetting the import line:

    io::printline("hello");
    // ERROR: I cannot find an enum, struct or module named `io`.

Even though `io` is a real module in the standard library, Turf won't know that unless you write `import io;` near the top of your file first.

A couple of other things to check if you did remember the import: is the name spelled exactly right (case matters)? And is `LeftName` actually a variable holding a value rather than a type name -- like `p::sum()` where `p` is a `Point` you already created? That's a slightly different mistake (you'd want `.` there instead of `::`), and Turf usually tells you that more specifically when it can recognize `p` as a variable.

Once the name before `::` is spelled correctly and (if it's a module) actually imported, this error clears up right away.

You used a type's short name, but it lives in another module

This one shows up when you write a bare type name like `List`, but `List` is actually declared inside a different module (say, `list`), and you never told Turf you wanted to use that short name directly. Turf won't guess -- it wants you to either spell out where the type comes from, or explicitly bring the short name into your file.

You've got two ways to fix this, and either one works:

    import list;
    mut l: list::List<int> = list::List::default();

That fully-qualified form needs the plain `import list;` (without it the error is E145, "module not imported"). Or, if you'd rather use the short name everywhere in the file:

    import list::List;
    mut l: List<int> = List::default();

Here's what triggers the error -- using the short name with neither of those:

    mut l: List<int> = List::default();
    // ERROR: I cannot use `List` here without qualification.

If you're not sure which module a type actually lives in, the error message tells you -- it names the owning module directly, so you can copy that name straight into either the qualified form or the import line.

This is really just Turf being explicit about where things come from, especially useful once your program starts pulling in several modules that might otherwise have similarly-named types.

You declared the same variable twice in the same scope

Once you've created a variable with `mut` or `immut`, you don't declare it again to change its value later -- you just assign to its name directly. This error means Turf found a SECOND `mut`/`immut` declaration for a name that's already in use, and it's not sure if you meant to make a new variable (in which case, use a different name) or just update the value of the one you already have.

This is the correct way to update a variable's value after it's declared:

    mut count: int = 0;
    count = count + 1;

And here's what triggers the error -- declaring `count` a second time instead of just assigning to it:

    mut count: int = 0;
    mut count: int = 5;
    // ERROR: I cannot declare `count` again, because it is already declared.

Turf will also point you back to exactly where the first declaration was, so you can see both at once.

The fix is almost always to drop the `mut`/`immut` and the type from the second line -- just write `count = 5;` instead. If you genuinely wanted two separate variables, give the second one its own distinct name instead.

One thing this is NOT: a variable declared again inside a nested block, like inside an `if` or a `while` loop, isn't this error -- that's a different (and totally normal) thing called shadowing, since the inner one lives in its own scope.

You declared two structs (or enums) with the same name

A struct, enum, or error type belongs to the file (or module) that declares it -- so inside one file, each type name can only be used once. Here's the mistake:

    struct Point { x: int, y: int }
    struct Point { x: double, y: double }
    // ERROR: I found a second struct named `Point`.

Different files are a different story: if a module you import also declares a `struct Config`, that's fine -- it's a separate type, `thatmodule::Config`, and your own `Config` stays yours. (If you import it by name, `import thatmodule::Config;`, a bare `Config` could mean either one -- that's a different error, E085, and Turf will name both.)

Turf will also point you to exactly where the first declaration of that name lives, so you can compare the two and decide what to do.

To fix it: rename one of the two declarations to something more specific.

You tried to transition into a state that belongs to a different machine

Turf's `machine` construct lets you declare a set of states and the moves allowed between them, like `machine ConnectionState { Idle -> Connecting, Connecting -> Connected }`. When you call `.transition(...)`, the thing inside the parentheses has to be one of THAT SAME machine's own states.

Here's a valid transition:

    machine ConnectionState {
        Idle -> Connecting,
        Connecting -> Connected,
    }

    mut conn: ConnectionState = ConnectionState::Idle;
    conn.transition(ConnectionState::Connecting);   // ConnectionState -> ConnectionState, matches

If you pass a state from a totally different machine, or something that isn't a state at all:

    machine LightState {
        Off -> On,
    }

    conn.transition(LightState::On);   // conn is a ConnectionState, not a LightState!

...Turf stops you, because `conn` can never actually become a `LightState` -- the two machines are unrelated types, even if their state names happen to look similar.

To fix it, double-check which machine's state you meant to use, and make sure it's a state defined on the exact machine type your variable was declared with. If the state name has a typo or doesn't exist on any machine at all, you'll actually see a different error first (an "unknown variant" error) -- this one specifically means the state exists, just on the wrong machine.

The same error is reported when the target has the right machine type but is a value rather than a written state, such as a parameter or variable that holds one:

    fn go(c: ConnectionState, next: ConnectionState): ConnectionState {
        mut conn: ConnectionState = c;
        conn.transition(next);             // which edge is this? It can't be known here
        return conn;
    }

A transition names its target, so the edge it takes is known where it is written. To move to a state chosen at run time, `compare` the value that chooses it and write one `.transition(ConnectionState::X)` per arm.

This kind of mix-up is easy to make once you have a couple of machines in the same file -- Turf catching it here means you find out immediately, not while debugging a confusing runtime issue later.

You defined a function inside another function

Turf doesn't allow "nested" functions -- a `fn` has to live at the top level of your file (or be attached to a struct as a method), never tucked inside another function's `{ }` body. If you write a function definition inside another one, the compiler will stop you here.

Here's the fix -- just pull the inner function out to the top level:

    fn outer(): int {
        return helper(2);
    }

    fn helper(x: int): int {
        return x * 2;
    }

This is different from what a lot of other languages allow (Python and JavaScript both let you define a function inside another one), so it's a really common thing to trip over early on if you're coming from one of those. In Turf, every function name lives in one flat, top-level list, so there's no real downside to moving it out -- it won't clash with anything just because it's no longer "hidden" inside another function.

If the function only makes sense in the context of a particular struct (like a helper that only operates on that struct's data), consider making it a method on the struct instead, declared with `fn StructName.method_name(...): ReturnType { ... }` and called as `instance.method_name(...)`.

You asked for a variant that doesn't exist on this enum (or error type)

Enums and `error` types in Turf only have the variants you actually listed when you declared them. This error means you wrote `TypeName::SomeVariant`, and `TypeName` is real and correctly declared, but `SomeVariant` isn't one of the names inside its `{ }` list.

Here's a correctly declared enum and a correct use of one of its variants:

    enum Color { Red, Green, Blue }
    immut c: Color = Color::Red;

And here's the mistake -- asking for a variant that was never declared:

    immut c: Color = Color::Purple;
    // ERROR: I cannot find a variant `Purple` in the enum `Color`.

Just like with struct fields, if your typo is close to a real variant name, Turf will usually suggest the one it thinks you meant.

The fix is to go back to the enum's (or `error` type's) own declaration and check the exact spelling of its variants -- they're case-sensitive, just like any other name in Turf. If the variant genuinely doesn't exist yet and you need it, you'll have to add it directly inside the type's own `{ }` list, since there's no separate way to extend one afterward.

This applies the exact same way to `error` types (declared with the `error` keyword instead of `enum`) -- they use the same variant-name rules under the hood.

Turf has never heard of this type name anywhere

This shows up whenever you write a type annotation -- on a variable, a function parameter, a return type, or a struct field -- and the type name you used doesn't match anything Turf knows about: not a struct or enum in your file, not one from any module you've imported, and not one of the built-in types like `int` or `string` either.

Here's a struct declared correctly and then used correctly:

    struct Point { x: int, y: int }
    mut p: Point = Point::default();

And here's the typo that trips this error:

    mut p: Piont = Point::default();
    // ERROR: I cannot find a type named `Piont`.

As usual, if your typo is close to a real type name, Turf will suggest the one it thinks you meant.

If it's not a typo, there are two other likely explanations. Maybe the struct or enum just hasn't been written yet -- double check you actually declared it somewhere in the file, above where you're using it. Or maybe it's meant to come from a module you haven't imported -- in that case, you'll usually see a slightly different, more specific error (about the type not being visible without qualification) instead of this one, which tells you exactly which module to import from.

Once the name matches a real, in-scope struct, enum, or builtin type exactly, this error goes away.

You marked your struct's constructor as static, but it can't be

`new` is the method that sets up a struct's fields when it's constructed -- its entire job is to fill in `self`. The `static` keyword means "this method has no `self` at all," which is the exact opposite of what `new` needs, so Turf won't let the two be combined.

Here's a `new` declared correctly, with no `static`:

    fn Point.new(x: int, y: int): void {
        self.x = x;
        self.y = y;
    }

Adding `static` to that same declaration is what triggers this error:

    fn Point.new(x: int, y: int) static: void {   // ERROR: I cannot make `new` `static`.
        self.x = x;
        self.y = y;
    }

The fix is simple: just delete the `static` keyword from the declaration. `new` is always allowed to use `self`, because filling in `self` is the whole point of having a constructor in the first place.

Your constructor doesn't set every field on every possible path

When you write a `.new` constructor, Turf checks that it always sets EVERY field of the struct, no matter which path the code takes through it -- not just "somewhere in the function," but on every single branch. If a field only gets assigned inside one half of an if/else, or only before some early returns and not others, Turf will catch that and tell you exactly which field it's worried about.

Here's a constructor that assigns both fields unconditionally, which is fine:

    fn Point.new(x: int, y: int): void {
        self.x = x;
        self.y = y;
    }

But here's one where a field is only set some of the time:

    fn Point.new(x: int, y: int): void {
        self.x = x;
        if x > 0 {
            self.y = y;
        }
    }

If `x` isn't greater than 0, `self.y` never gets a value -- and Turf would rather stop you here at compile time than let a struct silently end up with garbage/uninitialized memory in one of its fields. The error message tells you exactly which field(s) it couldn't prove were always assigned, so you know exactly what to fix.

The fix is almost always to make sure the field gets a value on every path -- for example, by assigning a sensible default before the `if`, or by also assigning it inside the `else` branch:

    fn Point.new(x: int, y: int): void {
        self.x = x;
        if x > 0 {
            self.y = y;
        } else {
            self.y = 0;
        }
    }

Once every field is guaranteed to be set no matter which way the code branches, this error goes away.

You called new() on a struct that never defined one

`StructName::new(...)` only works if that struct actually has a `.new` constructor written for it somewhere. Unlike calling a regular method with a typo, there's no "did you mean" fallback here -- a struct either has a `new` you can call with arguments, or it doesn't, and this error means yours doesn't (yet).

For example, this struct never declared a `.new`:

    struct Point {
        x: int,
        y: int
    }

    mut p: Point = Point::new(1, 2);   // ERROR: I cannot call `Point::new(...)`, because `Point` has no constructor.

You've got two good options here, depending on what you actually want:

If you want to construct it with specific starting values, write a `.new` for it:

    fn Point.new(x: int, y: int): void {
        self.x = x;
        self.y = y;
    }

If you just want an instance with every field zeroed out and don't need custom logic, you don't need to write anything at all -- every struct automatically gets a `::default()` you can call instead:

    mut p: Point = Point::default();

So if you're seeing this error and don't actually need custom construction logic, switching from `Point::new(1, 2)` to `Point::default()` (and then setting fields manually with `p.x = 1;` afterward, if needed) is often the quickest fix.

You tried to write your own default() method, but that name is reserved

Every struct in Turf automatically gets a `default()` constructor for free -- one that zeroes out every field -- and it's generated by the compiler itself. Because of that, `default` isn't a name you're allowed to declare a method under yourself; it's reserved specifically for the automatic one.

So writing something like this triggers the error:

    fn Point.default(): void {
        self.x = 0;
        self.y = 0;
    }
    // ERROR: I cannot accept a hand-written `default`, because it is reserved.

The good news is you don't need to write this at all -- `Point::default()` already works with zero code, doing exactly what you were trying to write by hand:

    mut p: Point = Point::default();

If what you actually wanted was custom construction logic -- something more specific than "zero everything out" -- that's exactly what `.new` is for instead. `new` is never reserved; you're expected to write it yourself:

    fn Point.new(x: int, y: int): void {
        self.x = x;
        self.y = y;
    }

So the fix is either: delete your `.default` entirely and just use the automatic one, or rename it to `.new` (and give it parameters) if you needed real custom logic.

You called a static method through an instance with '.'

A `static` method on a struct doesn't take a `self` at all -- it's not tied to any one instance, it belongs to the type itself. That means it should always be called with `::` on the struct's name, never with `.` on a variable you're holding, because `.` always implies "pass this instance in," and a static method has nowhere for that instance to go.

Here's a static method declared correctly, and called the right way:

    fn Point.origin() static: Point {
        return Point::default();
    }

    Point::origin();   // correct -- called on the type, with '::'

If you instead try to call it through an actual instance with `.`, Turf catches it:

    mut p: Point = Point::new(1, 2);
    p.origin();   // ERROR: `origin` is a static method, so I cannot call it through an instance with `.`.

The fix is just to swap `p.origin()` for `Point::origin()` -- use the type name instead of your variable. It's an easy mistake to make since ordinary (non-static) methods use `.` on an instance, and it's not always obvious from the call site alone whether a method was declared `static` or not -- if you're unsure, check the method's own declaration for the `static` keyword.

You put 'static' on a function that isn't attached to a struct

`static` is a keyword that only makes sense for a struct's method -- it means "this method doesn't take an implicit `self`." An ordinary, free-standing function was never going to receive a `self` in the first place, so putting `static` on one doesn't mean anything, and Turf rejects it.

Here's the difference between the two shapes: a struct method (where `static` is meaningful) looks like this:

    fn Point.origin() static: Point {
        return Point::default();
    }

...while an ordinary free function (where `static` doesn't apply at all) looks like this:

    fn add(a: int, b: int): int {
        return a + b;
    }

If you accidentally add `static` to that second kind, you'll see this error:

    fn add(a: int, b: int) static: int {   // ERROR: I cannot use `static` on a function that does not belong to a struct.
        return a + b;
    }

There are two ways to fix it, depending on what you meant: if this was always supposed to be a plain function, just delete `static`. If you actually meant for it to be a method belonging to a specific struct, add the `StructName.` prefix to the function's name so it's declared as a real struct method, like `fn Point.add(...) static: int { ... }`.

You tried to call a method that doesn't exist on this struct

This is the method-call sibling of the "unknown struct field" error -- same idea, just for something you're trying to call instead of something you're trying to read. Every struct only has the methods you actually declared for it (with `fn StructName.method_name(...)`), and if you call a name that isn't one of them, Turf stops you before your program runs with a call that goes nowhere.

The most common reason is a simple typo. Here's a struct with an `increment` method:

    fn Counter.increment(): void {
        self.val = self.val + 1;
    }

    mut c: Counter = Counter::default();
    c.increment();   // correct -- calls the real method

If you accidentally typed the method name wrong:

    c.incrament();   // typo!

...Turf will tell you exactly which method it thinks you meant, so you can just fix the spelling and move on, the same way it does for a misspelled field.

A couple of other things worth checking if it's NOT a typo:

  - Are you calling the method on the right variable? It's easy to mix up two structs that have similarly-named methods.
  - Did you mean to read a field instead? `c.val` and `c.val()` are different things -- one reads a stored value, the other tries to call it like a function. If `val` is a field, not a method, drop the parentheses.
  - Is the method declared `static`? A static method is called with `::` on the struct's type name, never `.` on an instance -- `Counter.increment()` would also fail this way if `increment` were static, since a static method isn't reachable through `.` at all.
  - If the method really should exist and just hasn't been written yet, declare it on the struct:

    fn Counter.reset(): void {
        self.val = 0;
    }

Once the method name matches exactly what the struct declares (and you're using `.` or `::` correctly for how it was declared), this error goes away and your code compiles like normal. Just like a misspelled field, this is a very normal thing to run into while you're still learning your way around a struct's methods -- nothing to worry about.

You passed a function an argument of the wrong type

Every Turf function has a fixed shape -- each parameter has its own declared type, and every call has to match that shape exactly. Turf checks this at compile time, before your program ever runs, so a mismatch like this can't sneak through.

Here's a function with a clear shape:

    fn greet(name: string, times: int): void {
        mut i: int = 0;
        while i < times {
            io::printline(name);
            i = i + 1;
        }
    }

    greet("Ada", 3);   // name gets a string, times gets an int -- matches

If you swap the arguments, or pass the wrong kind of value:

    greet(3, "Ada");

...Turf tells you exactly which parameter (by name, and by which argument position) got the wrong type, and what type it actually expects versus what you gave it.

The most common causes are simply passing arguments in the wrong order, or passing a value you assumed was one type when it's actually another. Since Turf doesn't automatically convert between types for you (a number won't silently become text, for example), double-check the function's declaration and match your call up to it argument by argument.

Your compare block isn't structured quite right

A `compare` block in Turf looks a bit like a switch statement: you give it a value, then list `matches <value>:` arms to handle each case, each one closed off with `done`. Turf found something wrong with the shape of this particular `compare` block -- not with the values or types involved, just with how the arms themselves are written.

A correctly structured compare looks like this:

    compare x {
        matches 1:
            result = 1;
        done
        matches 3:
            result = 3;
        done
        otherwise:
            result = 0;
        done
    }

This error usually comes from one of three mistakes. First, forgetting to close an arm with `done` before starting the next one:

    compare x {
        matches 1:
            result = 1;
        matches 2:
            result = 2;
        done
    }

Here, the first `matches 1:` arm never got its own `done` before `matches 2:` started, so Turf can't tell where one arm ends and the next begins.

Second and third: using more than one `otherwise:` arm, or putting `otherwise:` somewhere other than last. `otherwise` is your catch-all for "none of the above matched," so it only makes sense as the final arm -- Turf won't let you have two of them, or one in the middle with more arms after it.

The fix is almost always to slow down and check that every `matches` arm has its own `done`, and that if you're using `otherwise` at all, there's exactly one, at the very end. Once the shape is right, the error goes away and the block behaves exactly as you'd expect.

One of your compare arms doesn't match the type you're comparing against

A Turf `compare subject { matches ... }` block checks a value against a list of possibilities, and every single `matches` arm has to be the same type as `subject` itself.

Here, `code` is an `int`, so every arm is an `int` too:

    mut code: int = 404;
    compare code {
        matches 200:
            io::printline("OK");
        done
        matches 404:
            io::printline("Not Found");
        done
        otherwise:
            io::printline("Unknown");
    }

If one arm is written as text instead:

    matches "404":
        io::printline("Not Found");
    done

...Turf catches it, because `code` can never actually equal the string `"404"` -- it's an `int`, and `"404"` is a completely different type, not just a different-looking version of the same value.

To fix it, look at the compare subject's type first, then make sure every arm matches that exactly -- drop the quotes if the subject is a number, or add them if the subject is a string. It's an easy typo to make, especially when a number and its text form look almost identical.

You tried to change a variable that was declared as immutable

In Turf, when you declare a variable with `immutable` (or its shorter spelling `immut`), you're making a promise that its value will never change again after that line. Turf holds you to that promise at compile time.

This is fine -- `counter` was declared `mut`, so it's allowed to change:

    mut counter: int = 0;
    counter = counter + 1;

This isn't, because `pi` was declared `immut`:

    immut pi: double = 3.14159;
    pi = 3.14;

Turf tells you exactly which variable you tried to reassign, so there's no guessing which line caused it.

If you genuinely need this value to change later in your program, the fix is simple: go back to where it was declared and use `mut` (or `mutable`) instead of `immut`. If it really shouldn't change, then the assignment itself is the mistake -- you probably meant to introduce a brand new variable with a different name, rather than overwrite this one.

`immutable`-by-default thinking takes a little getting used to if you're coming from a language where every variable can always change, but it's a genuinely useful safety net: it means once you see `immut`, you know for certain that value is never going to shift under you later in the code.

The left side of your assignment isn't something you can actually assign to

`=` needs somewhere real to put the value -- a variable, a struct field, or a slot in an array. Those three are the only things Turf will let you write on the left side of an assignment.

All three of these are valid targets:

    mut x: int = 5;
    x = 10;                 // a variable

    mut p: Point = Point::default();
    p.x = 10;                // a struct field

    mut arr: int[3] = [1, 2, 3];
    arr[0] = 10;             // an array element

This isn't valid, because `true` is a fixed value, not a place to store something:

    true = 5;

Neither is this, because `(x + 1)` is a computed result, not a variable you can point at and overwrite:

    (x + 1) = 5;

Turf tells you exactly what was on the left side that it couldn't accept.

This error is almost always a sign of a typo or a mixed-up line -- maybe you meant to compare with `==` instead of assign with `=`, or you meant to assign to a variable but accidentally wrote an expression instead. Take a second look at what's actually on the left of the `=` and make sure it's a genuine variable, field, or array slot.

You returned a value that doesn't match the function's declared return type

When you write `fn square(n: int): int { ... }`, that `: int` at the end is a promise -- every `return` inside the function has to hand back a real `int`. Turf checks that promise at compile time.

This keeps the promise:

    fn square(n: int): int {
        return n * n;
    }

This breaks it:

    fn square(n: int): int {
        return "nine";
    }

Turf tells you both halves of the mismatch -- what the function is declared to return, and what you actually tried to return -- so you can see immediately which side needs to change.

There are two honest ways to fix this: change what you're returning so it's really the declared type (maybe you meant `n` or `n * n`, not a string), or change the function's own `: Type` if the value you're returning was correct all along and the declaration is what's actually wrong. Either is a completely normal thing to need while you're still shaping out a function -- it just means the signature and the body haven't quite agreed with each other yet.

A bare `return;` is the same mistake: in a function that promises an `int`, it hands back nothing. Write the value (`return 0;`), or declare the function as `void` if it really returns nothing.

You used a value where a different, incompatible type was expected

This is Turf's general type-mismatch error -- the one you'll see when a value's actual type doesn't line up with what was expected, and the mistake doesn't already have its own more specific, more detailed error message (function-call arguments and `return` values, for instance, have their own dedicated errors with more context).

The most common shape of this error is a variable's initializer not matching its declared type:

    mut n: int = 5;       // fine -- 5 is an int, matches the declaration
    mut bad: int = "five"; // string doesn't match int

But the same underlying check also covers a few related structural mistakes, like a `matches` pattern that's listed twice, an enum `compare` that doesn't handle every possible variant, a numeric cast that's out of range for the target type, or a struct literal that's missing one of its required fields. Whichever one it is, the message tells you which kind of problem you're looking at and spells out the specific details.

To fix it, read the message carefully -- it names the exact two types (or the exact missing piece) involved. From there it's usually one of two fixes: change the value so it's genuinely the type that was expected, or -- if the value was actually right and the *declared* type was the real mistake -- fix the declaration instead.

For a declaration, the hint names both fixes, because only you know which side you meant:

    mut count: int = true;
    // Hint: Declare `count` as `bool`, or give it an `int` value.

    mut label: string = 5;
    // Hint: Declare `label` as `int`, or convert the value with `to string`.

The value side offers a `to` only where a conversion exists (`5 to string`); otherwise it asks for a value of the declared type.

You tried to use the result of a function that doesn't return anything

A function declared with `: void` doesn't hand back a value when it finishes -- it just does something (like printing a message) and returns to whoever called it. Because there's genuinely no value produced, you can't store it, pass it along, or use it in a bigger expression.

Calling a `void` function on its own line is exactly what it's for:

    fn log_message(msg: string): void {
        io::printline(msg);
    }

    log_message("hello");   // fine -- just runs it

But trying to grab a value out of it doesn't work, because there isn't one:

    mut result: string = log_message("hello");

Turf tells you specifically that a void value was used where a real value was expected, along with a bit of context about where that happened.

There are two honest fixes here. If you only ever wanted the side effect (like the printed message) and never actually needed a value back, just call the function as its own statement, without trying to store or use the result. If you genuinely do need a value back, the real fix is upstream: change the function's own declared return type from `void` to whatever type it should produce, and add a `return` statement inside it that actually returns that value.

A number can't start or end with an underscore

Turf lets you use `_` inside a numeric literal to make long numbers easier
to read, the same way you might write "1,000,000" by hand:

    mut population: int = 1_000_000;
    mut pi_ish: double = 3.14_159;
    mut big_hex: int = 0xFF_FF_FF_FF;

The underscore only makes sense as a separator BETWEEN two digits. Put one
at the very start or the very end of a run of digits, and there's nothing
for it to actually separate:

    mut a: int = _5;     // ERROR: leading underscore
    mut b: int = 5_;     // ERROR: trailing underscore
    mut c: int = 0x_FF;  // ERROR: leading underscore right after the prefix

The fix is simply to remove the stray underscore:

    mut a: int = 5;
    mut b: int = 5;
    mut c: int = 0xFF;

If you were trying to group digits, make sure every underscore has a real
digit immediately on both sides of it.

This block of code can never run

Turf builds a map of every possible path your function's control flow can take, then checks which parts of your code that map can actually reach. This warning fires when it finds a whole block -- not just one statement, the entire block -- that no path leads to at all.

The classic way to end up here is a loop that always returns before it can exit normally:

    fn pick(): int {
        while true {
            return 1;
        }
        return 2;   // unreachable -- the loop above never exits any other way
    }

Since `while true` has no `break` and the only way out is the `return` inside it, control can never fall through to `return 2;`. That whole tail of the function is dead weight.

The fix depends on what you actually meant. If the second `return` should be reachable, give the loop a real exit:

    fn pick(): int {
        mut i: int = 0;
        while true {
            i += 1;
            if i > 10 {
                break;
            }
        }
        return 2;
    }

If the loop really is meant to run forever (or always return from inside), the trailing code is just leftover and safe to delete.

This is a warning, not an error -- Turf still compiles your program -- but unreachable code is almost always a sign the logic doesn't do what you think it does, so it's worth tracking down rather than ignoring.

There's a branch inside your code that can never actually be taken

This is a close cousin of "unreachable block" (E074): Turf found an `if` (or similar branch point) sitting inside code that's already dead, so the branch itself -- along with every path it could lead to -- can never run either.

It almost always shows up alongside E074, on the same piece of dead code:

    fn pick(x: int): int {
        while true {
            return 1;
        }
        if x > 0 {      // dead branch -- everything after the loop is unreachable
            return 2;
        }
        return 3;
    }

Because `while true` never exits any way other than its own `return`, nothing after the loop -- including the `if x > 0` check -- is ever reached. Turf reports the unreachable block itself (E074) and, separately, that the branch inside it is dead (E075), since a conditional that can never be evaluated is worth calling out on its own.

The fix is the same as for E074: either make the loop exit somewhere (a `break`, or a condition that isn't a literal `true`), or delete the code after it if it was never meant to run.

An integer literal is out of range for its destination type

Turf now checks every integer literal's EXACT value against the type it's
being given, instead of silently clamping or wrapping it:

    mut x: int = 999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999;
    // ERROR: The literal 999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999999 does
    // not fit in an `int`, which holds -9223372036854775808 .. 9223372036854775807

    mut y: int32 = 99999999999;
    // ERROR: too big for 'int32' -- it only holds up to 2147483647

This also fires for a compile-time expression built entirely out of
literals, once the two values combine into something out of range:

    mut c: int = 9223372036854775807 + 1;
    // ERROR: The literal 9223372036854775808 does not fit in an `int` (the sum
    // doesn't fit, even though each literal alone would)

'int' is a fixed 64-bit signed integer -- it's TurfLang's only
general-purpose integer type, and there's no wider one to promote to.
'int32' exists only for describing a C ABI honestly (`extern fn`
declarations) and is narrower still.

The fix is almost always one of:
  - Use a smaller literal.
  - If you're targeting 'int32' on purpose, double check the value fits
    32 bits, or use 'int' instead.
  - If you genuinely need arbitrary-precision arithmetic, that needs a
    library type -- it isn't a primitive in this language.

That value might be `null` when you use it

A reference written `&?Box` means "a Box, or nothing". The `?` is Turf's way of making you deal with "nothing" *before* it can crash your program: you may only read a field of a `&?Box` where Turf can prove it isn't `null`.

    struct Box { val: int }

    fn bad(b: &?Box): int {
        return b.val;            // b might be null here
    }

Check first, and use the value only inside the check:

    fn good(b: &?Box): int {
        if b != null {
            return b.val;        // here Turf knows b is not null
        }
        return 0;
    }

The proof only lasts as far as the check does: if the variable is changed in between, or the check was in a different branch, Turf asks again. A loop that does not assign the variable keeps the proof, inside it and after it.

Calling a method counts as a use, exactly like reading a field: `b.grow()` needs the same check.

A `defer`red use happens when the block ends, not where it is written: `defer io::printline(b.val);` followed later in the same block by `b = b.next;` is rejected, because `b` may be null by the time the deferred line runs.

The check works on a variable, and on a field reached from one (`n.next`, `n.next.next`):

    struct Node { val: int, next: &?Node }

    fn bad(n: &Node): int {
        return n.next.val;       // n.next might be null
    }

    fn good(n: &Node): int {
        if n.next != null {
            return n.next.val;
        }
        return 0;
    }

A check on a field lasts until the next function or method call (it may change any field it can reach) or the next write to a field with that name (another reference may point at the same struct). Read the field before calling anything, or give it a name: `immut r: &Node = n.next;` inside the check.

A `&?Box` you get from a call or a list element is checked by putting it in a variable first.

If a value can never be `null`, say so in its type: `&Box` (no `?`) needs no check at all.

You can't turn a maybe-`null` reference into a plain one without checking

`&?Box` is "a Box or nothing"; `&Box` is "definitely a Box". Converting the first into the second with `to &Box` is a promise that it isn't `null`, and Turf only accepts that promise where it can see it kept. Copying the value out with `to Box` reads through the reference, so it needs the same check.

    struct Box { val: int }

    fn bad(b: &?Box): int {
        mut safe: &Box = b to &Box;      // b might be null
        return safe.val;
    }

Check it first, and convert inside the check:

    fn good(b: &?Box): int {
        if b != null {
            mut safe: &Box = b to &Box;  // proven non-null here
            return safe.val;
        }
        return 0;
    }

Often you don't need the conversion at all: inside the `if b != null { ... }`, `b` can be used directly, and passed, returned or stored as a `&Box` as it is.

The same error is reported when a `&?Box` is used as a `&Box` without the check (`return b;` from a function that returns `&Box`, or `f(b)` for an `f` that takes one).

A variable can be checked, and so can a field reached from one (`h.item`), until the next call or the next write to a field with that name:

    struct Holder { item: &?Box }

    fn good(h: &Holder): int {
        if h.item != null {
            mut safe: &Box = h.item;
            return safe.val;
        }
        return 0;
    }

A `&?Box` from a call or a list element is stored in a variable first, then checked and converted.

A deferred statement can't `return`, `break` or `continue`

`defer` schedules one statement to run when the function ends, however it ends. That statement runs *while the function is already leaving*, so it can't also choose to leave in a different way:

    fn main(): int {
        defer return 5;          // return from where, exactly?
        return 0;
    }

`break` and `continue` have the same problem: they would jump out of the enclosing loop from the middle of the function's cleanup.

Keep the deferred statement to the cleanup itself -- close the file, free the buffer, print the message -- and put any decision about leaving in the normal flow of the function:

    fn main(): int {
        defer cleanup();
        return 5;
    }

If the cleanup itself can fail, handle that failure inside the deferred statement: `defer f() recover run { ... };`.

`caller_location` only works in a function that asks for it

`caller_location` is the file and line of whoever *called* this function. It is how a library function can say "you called me wrongly, here" instead of pointing inside the library. Because it costs a hidden extra argument on every call, a function has to opt in, by writing `@caller_location` on the line before `fn`:

    fn not_marked(): string {
        return caller_location;       // this function never asked for it
    }

Add the marker:

    @caller_location
    fn marked(): string {
        return caller_location;
    }

Or remove the use if you didn't mean to ask for the caller's position.

`@caller_location` goes on the line before `fn` (or before an `operator` declaration). It can share that position with the `@ownership(...)` and `@ffi(...)` annotations on a function.

A type name you used could mean more than one type

    import shapes::[*];          // shapes declares a `struct Point`

    struct Point { x: int }      // ...and so does this file

    fn main(): int {
        mut p: Point = Point::default();
        // ERROR: `Point` could mean more than one type here.
        //   Note: could be 'Point' declared in this program's main file
        //   Note: could be 'shapes::Point' from module 'shapes'
        return 0;
    }

A struct, enum, or error type belongs to the file or module that declares
it, so two files can each have their own `Point` -- they're separate types,
and neither declaration is a mistake. The problem is only the bare name:
`import shapes::[*];` (or `import shapes::Point;`) asked for shapes' `Point`
to be usable without the `shapes::` prefix, and this file declares a
`Point` of its own, so plain `Point` could mean either one. Turf won't
guess -- a program that silently picked "the local one" would change
meaning the day someone added an import.

To fix it, keep the bare name for one of them. Usually that's your own:
write the imported one qualified, `shapes::Point`, and leave `Point` out of
the import -- list the names you actually use instead of `[*]`:

    import shapes::[Circle, area];   // everything you need except Point

    struct Point { x: int }

    fn main(): int {
        mut mine: Point = Point::default();
        mut theirs: shapes::Point = shapes::origin();
        return 0;
    }

(Or rename your own type, if you'd rather use theirs bare.)

A reference to a local variable can't leave the function

A function's local variables live only until it returns; then that memory is reused. A reference (`&T`) into it would keep pointing at the reused memory -- a "dangling" reference -- so Turf refuses to let one escape: not as the return value, not stored into a list, not stored into a struct that outlives the call.

    struct Node { val: int }

    fn make(): &Node {
        mut n: Node = Node::default();
        n.val = 42;
        return &n;                 // n is gone when make() returns
    }

To hand a value back, copy it onto the heap with `heap(...)`. That copy is managed for you and lives as long as anything refers to it:

    fn make(): &Node {
        mut n: Node = Node::default();
        n.val = 42;
        return heap(n);
    }

A reference you *received* as a parameter is fine to return or store -- it already outlives this function. Only references into this function's own locals are the problem.

`pub` (or `public`) was written where it does nothing

    pub operator +(a: Money, b: Money): Money { ... }
    // ERROR: I cannot apply `pub` to an operator.
    //   Hint: Remove `pub` from the `operator` declaration, since an
    //   operator is visible wherever its operand types are. To let another
    //   module use `a + b` on your type, make the type itself `pub`.

`pub` (and its synonym `public`, spelled exactly like `mut`/`mutable`) marks
a declaration as visible to other modules. A `pub` that could never change
who can see something is rejected instead of quietly ignored -- an ignored
`pub` is how a typo turns into the belief "I exported that", which only
fails much later, in a different module.

Each place it can go wrong has its own message and its own fix:

    pub export immut LIMIT: int = 10;
        // `export` already IS the `pub` of a constant. Just remove `pub`.

    pub operator +(a: Money, b: Money): Money { ... }
        // Operators are visible wherever their operand types are. Remove
        // `pub`; make the TYPE `pub` if other modules should use it.

    pub import io;
        // Imports only affect the file that writes them and are never
        // re-exported. Remove `pub`; the other module writes its own import.

    pub immut LIMIT: int = 10;
        // A top-level constant is `export immut NAME: T = value;`.

    enum Color { Red, pub Green }
    error Oops { pub Bad }
    machine Light { pub Red -> Green }
        // A variant/state is public exactly when its type is:
        //     pub enum Color { Red, Green }

    fn add(pub a: int, b: int): int { ... }
        // Parameters are local to the function. To export the function,
        // put `pub` before `fn`:  pub fn add(a: int, b: int): int

    pub unsafe { ... }
        // Put `pub` on the declarations INSIDE the block:
        //     unsafe { pub fn f(): int { ... } }

    fn main(): int {
        pub struct Point { x: int }
        ...
    }
        // A declaration inside a function is local to it -- no other module
        // can ever see it. Remove `pub`, or move it to the top level.

    pub @caller_location
    fn here(): string { ... }
        // The decorator comes first, then `pub`:
        //     @caller_location
        //     pub fn here(): string { ... }

    mut pub: int = 1;
    fn public(): int { ... }
        // `pub` and `public` are reserved words now and can't be names.
        // Pick another, e.g. `is_public` or `published`.

Where `pub` IS meaningful: right before `fn`, `struct`, `enum`, `error`,
`machine` or `extern fn` at the top level of a file, and before a field
inside a struct body:

    pub struct Point { pub x: int, pub y: int, cache: int }
    pub fn Point.len(): int { ... }

A name you used belongs to another module, which did not mark it `pub`

    // util.tr
    pub fn api(): int { return helper() + 1; }
    fn helper(): int { return 41; }

    // main.tr
    import util;
    fn main(): int {
        return util::helper();
        // ERROR: I cannot use the function `helper`, which is private to
        //        module `util`.
        //   Note: declared in module `util` without `pub`
        //   Hint: Mark `helper` as `pub` in util.tr with `pub fn helper(...)`,
        //   if other modules are meant to use it. Otherwise, use one of the
        //   public functions that `util` offers: api.
    }

Everything a module declares at its top level -- functions, structs, enums,
errors, machines, extern fns -- is private to that module unless it is written
with `pub` (or `public`, the same word). Another module can only use the
`pub` ones. That keeps a module's helpers free to change, and makes its
public surface something you can read off the file.

The error appears however the private name is reached:

    util::helper()               // a qualified call
    mut h: util::Hidden = ...;   // a type annotation
    util::Hidden::default()      // constructing a private struct
    util::HiddenColor::A         // a variant of a private enum
    import util::helper;         // asking for it by name
    import util::[api, helper];  // ...in a list

Members of a `pub` type are checked the same way -- a struct's fields and
methods are private to its module unless written `pub`, even when the struct
itself is `pub`:

    // shapes.tr
    pub struct Point { pub x: int, pub y: int, cache: int }
    pub fn Point.len(): int { ... }
    fn Point.refresh(): void { ... }

    // main.tr
    p.x            // ok: a `pub` field
    p.cache        // ERROR: I cannot use the field `Point.cache`, which is private to module `shapes`.
    p.len()        // ok: a `pub` method
    p.refresh()    // ERROR: I cannot use the method `Point.refresh`, which is private to module `shapes`.
    Point::default()   // always fine -- it only zero-fills, so it cannot
                       // break an invariant that zero cannot

For a private field the hint names the type's public methods, because that is
usually how the data is meant to be reached: `List.data` is a raw pointer, so
`l.data[0]` says to use `get`, `set`, `length` and the rest instead.

Call syntax cannot get around it. `p.secret_twice()` resolves through
"uniform function call syntax" to a free function `secret_twice(p)` -- and a
free function another module did not mark `pub` is not a candidate there.

A `[*]` import (`import util::[*];`) is different: it just skips the private
names, so it never raises this error, and a private name in `util` can never
clash with one of your own.

There are two ways to fix it, and the hint offers both:

 1. The module is yours and the name is meant to be shared: add `pub` to the
    declaration in the module's own file --

        pub fn helper(): int { return 41; }

 2. The name is an implementation detail of somebody else's module: use what
    the module does offer. The hint lists its `pub` functions or types.

Two things that are NOT errors, because they never cross a module boundary:
a module using its own private names (`api()` above calling `helper()`), and
a `pub` generic -- function or struct method -- whose body uses a private
helper or a private field: when you instantiate it from your own file, the
copy is still checked as the module that wrote it.

`pub immut` constants are always public: `export` is their `pub`.

A public function or field mentions a type that is not public

    // shapes.tr
    struct Hidden { pub n: int }          // private: no `pub`
    pub fn make(): Hidden { ... }
    // ERROR: I cannot let the public function `make` expose the private type `Hidden`.
    //   Hint: Make the type public with `pub struct Hidden` in shapes.tr, or
    //   keep the function internal by removing its `pub`. Other modules would
    //   otherwise receive a `Hidden`, a type they cannot name.

A `pub` function, method, extern function or field is a promise to every
module that imports this one. If its signature or type names a private type
of the same module, an importer can still receive such a value -- from
`make()` above, or by reading the field -- while being unable to write the
type's name. That defeats the privacy the type asked for, so it is rejected
where the leak is written, not where it is used.

The type does not have to be the outermost one. All of these leak `Hidden`:

    pub fn a(x: Hidden): int { ... }            // a parameter
    pub fn b(): Hidden { ... }                  // the return type
    pub fn c(): !Hidden { ... }                 // inside `!T`
    pub fn d(x: &Hidden): int { ... }           // behind a reference
    pub fn e(): List<Hidden> { ... }            // as a generic argument
    pub struct Outer { pub inner: Hidden }      // a `pub` field
    pub fn Outer.f(h: Hidden): int { ... }      // a `pub` method

Two ways to fix it, and which is right depends on what you meant:

 1. Importers should be able to hold and name the type -- make it `pub`:

        pub struct Hidden { pub n: int }

 2. It is an implementation detail -- remove `pub` from the item that leaks
    it. A private function or a private field may use a private type freely:

        fn make(): Hidden { ... }

The same holds one level up, for a package. Other packages import only a
library's root module (src/<name>.tr), so a `pub` type of one of its internal
modules is a type they cannot name either. The root module may not put one in
a `pub` signature:

    // src/geometry.tr, the root module of package 'geometry'
    import vecmath;                       // an internal module
    pub fn origin(): vecmath::Vec { ... }
    // ERROR: I cannot let the public function `origin` of package `geometry`
    //   expose `Vec`, a type of its internal module `vecmath`.
    //   Hint: Move `pub struct Vec` into the root module, `geometry.tr`, or
    //   keep the function internal by removing its `pub`. Other packages
    //   import only `geometry`, its root module, so they would receive a
    //   `Vec`, a type they cannot name.

Declare the types of a library's API in its root module. An internal module's
own `pub` items may use its types freely: only the package imports them.

Not errors: a private type used inside a `pub` function's BODY; a `pub` method
of a private type (an importer cannot name the type, so it cannot call the
method); generic signatures over a type parameter (`pub fn<T> f(x: T): T`); a
private field of a private type in a `pub` struct; and, in a single-file
program, anything at all -- with no importer there is nobody to leak to.

A module exists in this build, but this package may not import it

    // turf.toml of myapp:  [dependencies] plotter = { path = "../plotter" }
    // plotter depends on geometry
    import geometry;
    // ERROR: I cannot import package `geometry`, because it is not a
    //        dependency of `myapp`.
    //     It is in this build because `plotter` depends on it.
    //   Hint: Add `geometry` to [dependencies] in `myapp`'s `turf.toml` to
    //   import it here. A package can import only its own modules, its
    //   direct dependencies and the standard library.

In a project, `import m;` can name exactly three kinds of module:

- one of your own package's modules (a `m.tr` anywhere under src/),
- a package you list under [dependencies], by its package name, or
- a standard-library module.

This error means `m` is in the build but is none of those three for the
package doing the import. It comes in two forms.

A transitive dependency. The package is built because something you depend
on depends on it, but you do not depend on it yourself. Using it anyway
would make your code break the day that dependency stops needing it, so add
it to your own [dependencies].

Another package's internal module. Every module of a package except its root
module (src/<package name>.tr) is internal to it:

    import shapes;
    // ERROR: I cannot import the module `shapes`, because it is internal to
    //        package `geometry`.

Import the package itself (`import geometry;`) and use what its root module
makes `pub`. If the package's author meant `shapes` to be part of its
interface, the root module is where they expose it.

This is a different error from "I cannot find a module named" (E027): here the file
exists, and a rule decides it is out of reach.

A `compare` doesn't cover every value of its subject

    enum Color { Red, Green, Blue }

    compare c {
        matches Color::Red:  ...  done
        matches Color::Green ...  done
    }
    // ERROR: This `compare` on `Color` does not cover every case, and it has no `otherwise`.
    //        Missing variants: Color::Blue
    //   Hint: Add an arm for each missing variant, or 'otherwise:' (or
    //   `matches _:`) to handle the rest.

With no `otherwise` and no unguarded `_`, these subjects must be covered
completely, because nothing else says what should happen to a value no arm
names:

- an enum or machine: every variant;
- a `bool`: both `true` and `false`;
- an error value (`err` in `recover as err run`): it can be any error a callee
  returns, so the set can't be listed and it always needs `otherwise` or `_`.

A guarded arm never counts toward coverage, however complete it looks:

    matches Color::Green if ready:    // ready may be false, so Green is
                                      // still not covered

An `int`, `string` or `char` subject is different: a `compare` over one may
match nothing, in which case it does nothing.

A value of a *declared* error type (`mut e: IOError = ...`, unlike the open
`err`) has a listable set of variants, so covering all of them is exhaustive
without `otherwise`: each by an unguarded variant pattern whose fields all match
anything (`IOError::Denied(_, mode)`). A variant whose pattern can fail
(`IOError::Denied(_, 0)`) is not yet covered.

A generic operator's type parameter has to be tied to its operands

A generic operator is written `operator<T> ...`: one definition that works for many types. Turf decides which `T` to use by looking at the operands at each use, so every type parameter has to appear in the operands, and at least one operand has to be a type that this module owns.

A parameter no operand mentions can never be decided:

    struct Cell<T> { v: T, n: int }

    operator<T> [](p: Cell<int>, i: int): T {    // T appears only in the result
        return p.v;
    }

Tie it to an operand:

    operator<T> [](p: Cell<T>, i: int): T {
        return p.v;
    }

And an operator whose operands are *all* bare `T` would redefine `+` for every type in the program, including ones this module doesn't own, so that is rejected too:

    operator<T> +(a: T, b: T): T { return a; }   // for which types?

Make at least one operand a type you declared -- `Cell<T>`, `Vec<T>`, your own struct.

That write would only change a copy

When you write `bs[0]`, the `[]` operator hands back the element *by value*: a copy. Assigning into a field of that copy would change the copy and throw it away, leaving the list untouched. Turf rejects it so the mistake can't silently do nothing:

    import list::List;

    struct Big { a: int }

    fn main(): int {
        mut bs: List<Big> = List::default();
        bs[0].a = 50;               // changes a copy of bs[0]
        return 0;
    }

Read the element into a variable, change that, and store it back:

    mut e: Big = bs[0];
    e.a = 50;
    bs[0] = e;

Writing *through* a pointer or reference element is fine, and so is writing into a nested list (`grid[i][j] = v`), because there the write lands on the real thing.

A semicolon is missing

Every statement in Turf ends with a semicolon `;`. This error means Turf finished reading one statement and found the start of the next one without a `;` in between.

    mut x: int = 5            // no semicolon
    return x;

Turf tells you the position where the *next* thing started, which is just after where the semicolon belongs. So look at the end of the line *above* the one it points to:

    mut x: int = 5;
    return x;

Where it applies: a variable declaration, an assignment, a function call on its own, `return`, `break`, `continue`, and the `;` after a `do { ... } while condition;` loop.

Where it doesn't: after a block's closing `}` for `if`, `while`, `for`, a function, a struct, an enum -- those end with the brace.

A bracket, brace or parenthesis was never closed

Every opening `(`, `[`, `{` and `<` needs a matching closing `)`, `]`, `}` and `>`. Turf got to a place where the closing one had to be, and it wasn't there.

    fn main(): int {
        mut total: int = add(1, 2;       // the ) is missing
        return total;
    }

Put the missing one back:

    mut total: int = add(1, 2);

For a missing `}` Turf points at the `{` that was never closed, not at the end of the file, so you can see which block is unfinished:

    fn main(): int {
        if true {
            return 1;
        // <- the } for the `if` is missing here
        return 0;
    }

Inside a list -- array items, struct fields, call arguments -- the message may instead say it expected "`,` or `]`". That is E105: from that spot Turf can't tell a missing comma from a missing closing bracket, so it names both. Look at what comes next: if it is another item, add the comma; if the list was meant to end, add the bracket.

Two habits make this rare: type the closing character as soon as you type the opening one, and let an editor with bracket matching show you which brackets pair up.

A colon is missing

In Turf a colon `:` joins a name to its type, and a function's parameter list to its return type. Turf expected one and found something else.

The commonest case is forgetting the colon before a function's return type:

    fn add(a: int, b: int) int {     // needs `:` before `int`
        return a + b;
    }

    fn add(a: int, b: int): int {
        return a + b;
    }

A function that declares no return type at all reports the same error: every function says what it returns, `: void` when it returns nothing. The compiler tries `void`, the basic types and the types of the function's parameters, and when exactly one of them compiles with the body and the function's callers, it prints that as a `Fix:`.

The same shape shows up for a parameter or a struct field, which is `name: type`:

    fn greet(name string) { }        // should be `name: string`
    struct User { age int }          // should be `age: int`

And in `compare`, the `matches` and `otherwise` arms need a colon before their body:

    otherwise: { ... }

An opening brace or parenthesis is missing

Turf expected `{` to start a block (or `(` to start a parameter list) and found something else.

In Turf the body of a function, `if`, `elseif`, `while`, `for`, `do`, `unsafe`, `compare`, `struct`, `enum` and `machine` is *always* wrapped in braces -- there is no single-statement shortcut:

    if x > 0 return 1;           // no braces

    if x > 0 {
        return 1;
    }

The same goes for a function name that isn't followed by its parameter list:

    fn main: int { ... }         // needs ()

    fn main(): int { ... }

If you want a one-line choice between two values, use the ternary form instead of a braceless `if`: `give a otherwise b`.

Nested too deeply

Turf reads your code by following its structure: a bracket inside a bracket inside a bracket. To stay safe, it stops at a fixed depth instead of following forever, because input that nests without end can crash a compiler.

    mut x: int = ((((((((((((... hundreds of parentheses ...))))))))))));
    mut y: bool = !!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!!...;
    mut z: list<list<list<list<...>>>> ...;

Real programs never get close to the limit, so when you see this it almost always means a bracket (or `<`, or `!`) was typed over and over by accident, or a closing one is missing and the count keeps growing. Check the line it points to for a repeated character and delete the extras.

If you genuinely have deeply nested data, give the inner pieces names with `mut` and build the result step by step.

The same name is declared twice in one declaration

Inside a struct, an enum, an `error` type or a `machine`, every member needs its own name. Turf found a second member with a name that was already taken, and with two of them it couldn't tell which one you meant.

    struct User {
        name: string,
        name: int            // `name` is already a field
    }

    enum Color { Red, Green, Red }

    machine Door { Open -> Closed, Open -> Closed }     // the same transition twice

Rename one, or delete the extra one:

    struct User {
        name: string,
        age: int
    }

(This is about *members*. Declaring two variables with the same name in one scope is a different mistake -- see E033.)

An operator was declared with the wrong shape

An `operator` function teaches Turf what a symbol like `+` or `[]` means for your own type. Each operator has a fixed number of operands, and Turf checked yours against it.

    fn operator+(a: Vec, b: Vec, c: Vec): Vec { ... }   // `+` takes two operands
    fn operator[](self: Grid): int { ... }              // `[]` takes (self, index)
    fn operator[]=(self: Grid, i: int): void { ... }    // `[]=` takes (self, index, value)

Binary operators (`+ - * / % ** & | ^ << >> == != < > <= >=`) take exactly two parameters. A few have only one form: `!` and `~` are unary (one parameter), and `-` can be either. The message tells you what the operator you wrote needs and how many you gave.

Fix the parameter list to match:

    fn operator+(a: Vec, b: Vec): Vec { ... }

An `extern` function can't have a body

`extern fn` doesn't define a function, it *announces* one that already exists somewhere else (usually in C). The code is not in your file, so there is nothing to write in `{ ... }`:

    extern fn malloc(size: int): ptr {       // no body allowed
        return null;
    }

Declare it with a semicolon instead:

    extern fn malloc(size: int): ptr;

If you wanted to write the function yourself in Turf, drop the `extern`:

    fn my_alloc(size: int): ptr { ... }

An `error` declaration mixes two forms

An `error` type can be written in two ways, and a declaration has to pick one.

A bare error, with no variants -- it ends with a semicolon:

    error NotFound;

An error with named variants -- it uses braces and no semicolon in front of them:

    error IOError { NotFound, PermissionDenied, Closed }

Writing the bare `;` form and then also a `{ ... }` variant list right after it is ambiguous -- is the type finished, or isn't it? -- so Turf rejects it:

    error NotFound; { Missing }

Keep whichever you meant and delete the other.

Statements have to live inside a function

A Turf file can declare things at its top level -- functions, structs, enums, imports, `extern` bindings, `pub immut` constants -- but it can't *do* anything there. Statements (a call, an assignment, a variable, a `return`, an `if`) must be inside a function.

    import io;

    io::printline("hello");          // a call, outside any function

    fn main(): int { return 0; }

Move it into `main`, which is where a program starts:

    import io;

    fn main(): int {
        io::printline("hello");
        return 0;
    }

If a file has statements at the top level because it was meant to be a script, wrap them in `fn main(): int { ... return 0; }`.

A top-level `unsafe { }` block is the same: it can wrap declarations (a struct with a raw-pointer field, an `fn`), but a statement inside it is still outside any function. Put the statement in a function, inside an `unsafe { }` of its own.

That operation isn't supported in this form

Some shorthand forms don't work in places where the longer way does. The message says which form and shows the longer way to write it.

`int32` is a storage type: arithmetic on it produces an `int`, never another `int32`, so the shorthand that would store the result straight back isn't available:

    mut x: int32 = 5 to int32;
    x += 1;                       // not supported on int32

Write the arithmetic out and convert the result back yourself:

    x = (x + 1) to int32;

The same applies to `x++` and `x--` on an `int32`.

Compound assignment through an `operator[]` that you defined (`grid[i] += 1`) isn't supported either. Write the read and the write out explicitly:

    grid[i] = grid[i] + 1;

Indexing something that can't be indexed is the same error: `[ ]` works on arrays, strings, pointers, references (`&T`), and on a type that defines an `operator []`. A `&?T` that may be null is checked and converted to a `&T` first. Anything else, such as an `int`, has no positions to look at:

    mut n: int = 3;
    return n[0];                  // an int has no [0]

If you meant to look inside a type of your own, give it an `operator []`:

    operator [](b: Box, i: int): int {
        return b.value + i;
    }

Reading through a type that only has `operator []=` is the same error. The setter lets you write `box[0] = 5`, but a read needs the getter:

    return box[0];                // IntBox declares operator []=, not operator []

Declare `operator []` as well. A getter that takes a different index type than the one you passed ("No 'operator[]' overload matches") is reported separately; the index type must match exactly.

`void` has no size, so `sizeof(void)` isn't supported. Use `sizeof(void*)` for the size of a pointer, or the size of the type you actually mean:

    malloc(sizeof(void));         // not supported
    malloc(sizeof(int));          // fine

Raw pointers can only be used inside `unsafe`

A raw pointer (`ptr`, or `int*`, `Point*`, ...) is a memory address with none of Turf's safety checks: it can be null, it can point at something that has been freed, and it can be read past its end. Turf only lets you use one inside an `unsafe { }` block, where you are saying "I am taking responsibility for this".

    fn main(): int {
        mut p: ptr = null;            // not allowed out here
        return 0;
    }

Put the code that needs it inside `unsafe`:

    fn main(): int {
        unsafe {
            mut p: ptr = null;
            ...
        }
        return 0;
    }

Or avoid raw pointers altogether. A safe reference, `&T`, covers almost everything a beginner needs a pointer for, and `&?T` is the version that may be `null`.

A raw pointer also can't appear in an ordinary function's signature -- not even inside `unsafe`. Use `&T` for parameters and return values; only `extern fn` declarations (bindings to C) may use raw pointers there.

A `to` cast between a reference and something that is not a reference to the same type needs `unsafe` too: making a reference (`0 to &N`, `s to &N`, `r to &M`) may point it at nothing, and `r to string` reads the referenced bytes as text instead of formatting the value. Convert a field instead: `r.x to string`.

`recover` is being used in a way that can't work

`recover` handles a call that can fail. It has two rules, and Turf found one broken.

**It needs something that can fail.** Only a call whose declared return type starts with `!` -- `fn read(): !string` -- can be followed by `recover`. An ordinary call has nothing to recover from:

    fn f(): int { return 1; }
    mut v: int = f() recover produce 0;       // f() can't fail

Drop the `recover`, or make the function fallible if it really can fail: `fn f(): !int`.

**A `recover run { ... }` block has to leave.** When it doesn't `produce` a value, the expression it is attached to has no value to become, so every path through the block must end in `return`:

    mut text: string = read("a.txt") recover run {
        io::printline("couldn't read it");
    };                                         // falls out the bottom: no value

    mut text: string = read("a.txt") recover run {
        io::printline("couldn't read it");
        return 1;
    };

Or supply the value directly: `recover produce ""`.

A module name can't be qualified by another module

`module::function(...)` reaches into one module. Modules aren't nested inside each other, so there is no `a::b::function` -- you have written two module names in a row.

    import io;
    import mem;

    mem::io::printline("hi");           // `io` is not inside `mem`

Use the one module that actually has the function:

    io::printline("hi");

If you meant a type or an enum inside a module, that is the one form with three parts: `module::Type::Variant`, e.g. `shapes::Color::Red`. The check only complains when the middle name is itself a module.

A function can't be used as a value here

A function used as a value is written by its bare name: `mut f: fn(int): int = twice;`. A qualified name such as `io::printline` or `Point::origin` is only ever called, with parentheses and arguments:

    import io;

    mut f: fn(string): void = io::printline;   // a qualified name is not a value

    io::printline("hello");                     // a call: this runs it

To use a module's function as a value, import it by name and write the bare name:

    import helper::twice;

    mut f: fn(int): int = twice;

A static method (`Point::origin`) has no value form. Wrap the call in a plain function and use that function's name:

    fn origin(): Point { return Point::origin(); }

An `@caller_location` function is not a value either (only a direct call passes the location). Wrap the call in a plain function the same way.

A generic function is a value only where something says what its type parameters are: a function type, or type arguments written after `::`:

    fn<T> id(x: T): T { return x; }

    mut g = id;                       // E137: nothing says what T is
    immut f: fn(int): int = id;       // fine: T is int
    immut h = id::<int>;              // fine: T is int

A generic type needs its type arguments

A generic type is a template: `Box<T>` or `List<T>` becomes a real type only when you say what `T` is. Naming it on its own leaves Turf not knowing what the box holds.

    struct Box<T> { v: T }

    mut b: Box = Box::default();            // a Box of what?

Say what it holds:

    mut b: Box<int> = Box::default();
    mut names: List<string> = List::default();

The same rule applies to a method of a generic struct. It repeats the struct's type parameters, so Turf knows they are the same ones:

    struct Box<T> { v: T }
    fn<T> Box.get(): T { ... }              // not `fn Box.get()`

An unsized array type (`int[]`) was used somewhere it can't safely live

`int[]` -- an array type with nothing inside the brackets -- means "an array of any length". It exists for one job: a function parameter that should accept arrays of different sizes.

    fn sum(arr: int[]): int {          // takes an int[3], an int[10], a literal...
        mut total: int = 0;
        for value in arr {
            total = total + value;
        }
        return total;
    }

Such a parameter doesn't get its own copy of the array. It gets a view of the caller's array: where the elements are, and how many there are. That's what makes it work for any length. A local variable can be a view too (`immut tail: int[] = arr[1..];`, or a literal, `immut nums: int[] = [10, 20, 30];`), because a local never outlives its function. A view is only safe while the array it looks at still exists, which is guaranteed inside one function and nothing longer. So everywhere that would keep it longer, Turf asks for a real size instead:

    struct Bag {
        values: int[]                  // E140 -- a field needs a size (or use List<int>)
    }

    fn make(): int[] { ... }           // E140 -- return int[3], or a List<int>

    fn f(rows: int[3][]): int { ... }  // E140 -- only the FIRST dimension may be empty:
                                       //   int[][3] (any number of rows of three) is fine

    mut rows: int[2][] = [[1], [2]];   // E140 -- the same rule for a local variable

    fn g(r: &int[]): int { ... }       // E140 -- just write r: int[]; it already shares

    extern fn c_sum(buf: int[], n: int): int;   // E140 -- C has no such type;
                                                //   take a T* and a length instead

The same goes for a type that would hold one: `List<int[]>`, a function type like `fn(int[]): int`, a cast `x to int[]`, and a generic call that would make a type parameter `T` stand for `int[]` (for example `keep(arr)` with `fn<T> keep(x: T): T`).

To fix it, give the array a size (`int[5]`), use a `List<T>` when the number of elements changes, or, if you want a function to accept any length, make it a parameter. A local view needs an initializer (E128 otherwise). For a generic function, write the parameter as `T[]` rather than `T`: `fn<T> show(arr: T[])` called with an `int[4]` makes `T` the element type, `int`.

An immutable array was passed to a function that changes it

A parameter written `arr: int[]` doesn't get a copy of your array. It works on your array directly, which is what lets a helper like this one fill an array of any length in place:

    fn fill(arr: int[], value: int): void {
        for (i, _old) in arr {
            arr[i] = value;
        }
    }

    fn main(): int {
        mut a: int[3] = [1, 2, 3];
        fill(a, 0);                    // a is now [0, 0, 0]
        return 0;
    }

That means passing an `immut` array to such a function would change something you said can never change, so Turf stops you:

    fn main(): int {
        immut a: int[3] = [1, 2, 3];
        fill(a, 0);                    // E141 -- 'a' is immutable, but 'fill'
        return 0;                      //   writes to the array it is given as 'arr'
    }

The check looks at what the function actually does with that parameter: assigning to an element (`arr[i] = v`, `arr[i] += v`, `arr[i]++`), calling a method that changes an element (`arr[i].bump()`), or handing the array on to another function that does one of those. A function that only reads the array is fine to call with an `immut` one; `sum(a)` compiles. An array field of an `immut` struct counts as immutable too (`clear(h.data)`). So does a slice of an immutable array (`fill(a[1..], 0)`) and an `immut` slice variable (`immut v = m[..]; fill(v, 0);`). Inside the function, writing through a local view of the parameter (`mut rest: int[] = arr[1..]; rest[0] = 0;`) counts as writing the parameter.

To fix it, declare the array with `mut` if it's meant to change. If it isn't, and you only want the function's result, copy the array into a `mut` one and pass the copy:

    immut a: int[3] = [1, 2, 3];
    mut scratch: int[3] = a;
    fill(scratch, 0);                  // a is untouched

or pass `a.copy()`, a new independent array, straight to the function (`fill(a.copy(), 0);`).

A parameter with a size, like `arr: int[3]`, is the other way round: it always gets its own copy, so changes inside the function never reach the caller.

A range slice `[a..b]` was used on something that isn't an array

`x[1..3]` takes part of an array: a view of the elements from 1 up to (not including) 3. It only makes sense for a built-in array (`int[5]`, a row of a 2D array, an `int[]` parameter or another slice). Anything else is refused:

    immut s: string = "hello";
    immut t = s[1..3];                 // E142 -- a string is not an array

    mut l: List<int> = List::default();
    immut u = l[0..1];                 // E142 -- a List keeps its elements behind its own buffer

    immut n: int = 5;
    immut v = n[0..1];                 // E142 -- an int has no elements

A string is text that ends at a zero byte, so a view into its middle would have no end marker. To take part of a string, build a new string with `strings::substring(s, start, end)`. To take part of a List, copy the elements you need in a loop (or into an array).

The same applies to a struct: `p[0..1]` on a struct value is E142. Indexing a single element, `l[i]`, still works on a List; only the range form is limited to arrays.

A `mut` view was taken of an immutable array

A slice (`a[1..]`) or an `int[]` variable doesn't copy the array it looks at: it shares it. Writing through a `mut` view changes the original, which is useful:

    mut a: int[5] = [1, 2, 3, 4, 5];
    mut view: int[] = a[1..3];
    view[0] = 20;                      // a[1] is now 20

For an `immut` array that would change something you said can never change, so Turf stops you:

    immut a: int[3] = [1, 2, 3];
    mut t: int[] = a[1..];             // E144 -- 't' is a 'mut' view of 'a', which is immutable
    mut u = a[..];                     // E144 -- the same, with the type inferred
    mut w: int[] = a;                  // E144 -- a whole array is a view too

It is refused again when an existing `mut` view is pointed at an immutable array later:

    mut scratch: int[3] = [4, 5, 6];
    mut view: int[] = scratch[..];
    immut fixed: int[3] = [1, 2, 3];
    view = fixed[1..];                 // E144 -- 'view' is a 'mut' view of 'fixed'

To fix it, declare the view `immut` if you only read it (`immut t = a[1..];`), or make the array `mut` if it is meant to change. If you want to change a copy, take an editable copy with `.copy()`, which is a new array and not a view, so it is allowed:

    immut a: int[3] = [1, 2, 3];
    mut c = a.copy();                  // an independent int[]
    c[0] = 9;                          // a is untouched

A module is used, but this file never imported it

A file may write `m::something` only for a module it imports itself. Another module that happens to import `m` does not count:

    import json;                       // json itself imports list, so list is loaded...
    fn main(): int {
        mut l: list::List<int> = list::List::default();   // E145 -- ...but this file never imported it
        return 0;
    }

The fix is the line the error prints:

    import list;
    import json;

It means the file keeps working if `json` stops importing `list` one day, and a reader can see where every `module::` in the file comes from.

The modules every program already has -- `int`, `double`, `bool`, `char`, `strings`, `array`, `io`, `math` and `sys`, the prelude -- are the exception: `io::printline("hi")`, or just `printline("hi")`, needs no import at all.

An import of one name is not an import of the module. `import list::List;` lets you write `List`, but not `list::List`; for the qualified spelling the file needs its own `import list;`, and if it uses both spellings it has both lines:

    import list;
    import list::List;

    fn main(): int {
        mut a: list::List<int> = list::List::default();
        mut b: List<int> = List::default();
        return 0;
    }

What is not affected: you can still hold, pass and return a value whose type comes from a module you did not import, call its methods (`b.get()`), call a free function on it through the dot (`b.twice()`), and use its operators (`b + b`). Those follow the value's type. Only *spelling* `module::name` needs the import.

This is a different mistake from a misspelled module name (a typo, which gets a "did you mean" suggestion): here the module exists, and the file just has not asked for it.

An annotation the declaration under it cannot carry

    @ownrship(acquires)
    pub extern fn malloc(size: int): void*;
    // ERROR [E146]: I do not know an annotation named `@ownrship`.
    //   Hint: Write `@ownership` instead, or use one of the known annotations: @ownership, @ffi.

An annotation, `@name(arguments)` written on the line before a function or an
`extern fn`, tells the compiler a fact about that declaration. The compiler
knows what a function does only because its declaration says so, never because
of the function's name: `malloc` is understood as an allocator because it says
`@ownership(acquires)`, and your own allocator is understood the same way when
it says the same thing.

That is why a wrong annotation is an error rather than something quietly
skipped. A misspelt one would otherwise switch off the very check it was
written to turn on, and you would never find out.

There are three ways to get this error.

The name is not one the compiler knows. The message suggests the closest one
and lists them all:

    @ownership(acquires)                  // the double-free / use-after-free check
    @ffi(macos: malloc_size)              // which C symbol an extern binds to, per platform

The arguments do not fit the declaration. The message says what is wrong:

    @ownership(acquires)
    fn count(): int { return 0; }
    // '@ownership(acquires)' says the function returns a fresh allocation,
    // but it does not return a pointer.

    @ownership(releases: q)
    pub extern fn free(p: void*): void;
    // 'q' is not a parameter of this function -- write 'releases: p'.

    @ffi(macos: malloc_size)
    fn helper(): int { return 0; }
    // '@ffi' can only sit on an 'extern fn'.

A `@test` is malformed. `@test` marks a top-level function as a test (see the
language reference, `@test`): it takes no parameters, returns `void` or `!void`,
and may start with an outcome (`compile_error`, `warning`, `no_warnings`,
`panic`, `exit`, `error`), followed by options such as `timeout: 2000`. The
message names the rule and the hint lists the outcomes and the options:

    @test(compile_eror, E041, "x")
    fn t(): void { }
    // 'compile_eror' is not an outcome of '@test'.

    @test(compile_error, E999, "x")
    fn t(): void { }
    // 'E999' is not a code the compiler has.

    @test(compile_error, E172, "x")
    fn t(): void { }
    // 'E172' is a warning's code, not an error's.

    @test
    fn t(n: int): void { }
    // A test takes no parameters.

    @test
    fn t(): int { return 1; }
    // A test returns 'void' or '!void', not 'int'.

    @test(exit, 256)
    fn t(): void { }
    // '256' is not an exit status.

    @test(error, ParseError::Gone)
    fn t(): !void { return; }
    // The error type `ParseError` has no variant `Gone`.

    @test(retries: 3)
    fn t(): void { }
    // `@test` has no option `retries:`.

    @test(timeout: 0)
    fn t(): void { }
    // `timeout:` takes a whole number of milliseconds greater than 0.

Also rejected: an outcome that is not the first argument, a method or `main`
as a test, a second `@test` on one function, an option written twice, a
`timeout:` or `stdout:` on a `compile_error` test, and an `error` outcome on a
function that does not return `!void`. The options are `skip:`, `timeout:`,
`levels:`, `stdout:`, `tag:` and `serial`, after the outcome. A `@test`
function nested in another function is E038.

The fix is to correct the spelling, or to say what is actually true of the
declaration, or to remove the annotation.

A malformed annotation -- a missing ')' or an expression such as `1 + 2` in the
arguments -- is a syntax error instead (E103), reported where the parser stopped.
An argument may be a word, a string, a whole number or a `Type::Variant` path.

A type has no default value because it reaches itself through `&` fields

`T::default()` gives every field its default: 0, false, "", an empty collection. A `&U` field can never be null, so its default is a new `U::default()` of its own, made on the heap. That is what lets every collection share one header from the moment it is created.

A type that reaches itself through `&` fields cannot be defaulted this way, because each new value would need another new value inside it, forever:

    struct Node {
        v: int,
        next: &Node
    }

    fn main(): int {
        mut n: Node = Node::default();   // E147 -- 'Node' reaches itself through '&' fields
        return n.v;
    }

The cycle can be longer (`A` has a `&B`, `B` has a `&A`), and it can pass through fields that hold a struct by value; the error names the type you defaulted and the type that repeats.

Two ways out:

1. Make one field on the cycle a nullable reference, `&?Node`. It defaults to null, which ends the chain; check it with `if (n.next != null)` before using it:

    struct Node {
        v: int,
        next: &?Node
    }

    fn main(): int {
        mut n: Node = Node::default();   // fine: next is null
        return n.v;
    }

2. Build the value with a constructor, `Node::new(...)`, and pass it the `&Node` it should point to.

A `&U` field that does not lead back to its own type is fine: `struct Holder { h: &Counter }` defaults to a fresh `Counter::default()`.

A type was given the wrong number of type arguments

A generic struct declares its type parameters, and every use of it gives exactly one type for each: `struct Box<T>` takes one, `struct Pair<A, B>` takes two. A struct with no type parameters, or an enum, takes none.

    struct Point { x: int, y: int }
    struct Box<T> { v: T }

    mut p: Point<int> = Point::default();     // Point is not generic
    mut b: Box<int, int> = Box::default();    // Box takes one type

Write each type the way it was declared:

    mut p: Point = Point::default();
    mut b: Box<int> = Box::default();

Giving a generic struct no type arguments at all, `mut b: Box = ...`, is E138.

That text can never be converted

`to int`, `to double` and `to Enum` convert a string while the program runs, and they panic when the text is not a number (or not a variant name). When the string is written right there in the code, Turf can tell in advance that the conversion would always panic, so it stops you now:

    enum Color { Red, Green }

    mut n: int = "abc" to int;          // "abc" is not an int
    mut d: double = "1.2.3" to double;  // two decimal points
    mut c: Color = "Purple" to Color;   // Color has no Purple

The whole string must be the value. `to int` takes an optional `-` and digits, in int's range, so `"12abc"`, `" 12"`, `"+5"` and `"1.5"` are all rejected too. `to double` also takes one `.` and an exponent (`"2.5"`, `"1e3"`), but not `"inf"` or `"nan"`. A variant name must match exactly, including its case.

Usually the fix is to write the value itself instead of text:

    mut n: int = 42;
    mut d: double = 1.5;
    mut c: Color = Color::Green;

For text that comes from outside the program, such as `input()` or a file, use `strings::parse_int` or `strings::parse_double`, which return an error you can `recover` from instead of panicking.

A size parameter used where it can't be

A generic function can take an array's length as a parameter, written `N: size`:

    fn<T, N: size> fill(value: T): T[N] {
        mut r: T[N];
        for i in 0..N {
            r[i] = value;
        }
        return r;
    }

    mut zeros: int[8] = fill(0);   // N is 8, from the type the result goes into

N is fixed by each call, so it has a few rules, and E150 is breaking one of them:

    struct Buffer<N: size> { used: int }             // only a free function can have one
    fn<N: size> S.first(a: int[N]): int { ... }      // not a method or an operator
    fn<N: count> first(a: int[N]): int { ... }       // the only kind after ':' is size
    fn<N: size> first(a: N, b: int[N]): int { ... }  // N is a length, not a type
    fn<N: size> first(N: int, a: int[N]): int { ... } // a parameter can't share its name
    fn<N: size> count(a: int[N]): int { N = 3; ... } // N can't be changed

Inside the function, N reads as an int, as in `for i in 0..N`. To change the number,
copy it first: `mut n: int = N;`. For a struct that holds several values, give it a
fixed-size array field (`items: int[8]`) or a List.

A generic struct declared inside a function

A struct can be declared inside a function body, for a type only that function uses:

    fn area(w: int, h: int): int {
        struct Rect { w: int, h: int }
        mut r: Rect = Rect::default();
        r.w = w;
        r.h = h;
        return r.w * r.h;
    }

Such a local struct can't have type parameters. A local type has no methods, so a
type parameter would have nothing to use it, and E151 is writing one anyway:

    fn f(): int {
        struct Box<T> { v: T }   // E151
        ...
    }

Declare the generic struct at the top level of the file instead, where its methods
can be written too, or give the local struct's fields concrete types.

This variable hides another one with the same name

Declaring a variable with the same name as one that is already visible creates a brand-new variable that takes over the name for the rest of its scope. The older one is still there, you just can't reach it by name any more:

    fn main(): int {
        immut count: int = 1;
        if count > 0 {
            immut count: int = 2;   // WARNING: This new `count` hides an older one.
            printline(count to string);
        }
        return count;
    }

Inside the `if`, `count` is `2`; outside it, `count` is `1`. Nothing is wrong with that, but a reader (or you, in a month) has to work out which `count` each line means, and an edit that moves a line across the brace can quietly switch which one it sees.

The usual fix is a different name for the inner variable:

    fn main(): int {
        immut count: int = 1;
        if count > 0 {
            immut inner: int = 2;
            printline(inner to string);
        }
        return count;
    }

If the inner variable really is a fresh value that deliberately takes over the name, the warning is only a nudge and can be allowed.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E152` (or `[lints] E152 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

This `compare` has no arms

A `compare` picks an arm by matching a value against its `matches` headers. One with no arms and no `otherwise` has nothing to pick, so it does nothing at all:

    immut n: int = 1;
    compare n {
    }                      // WARNING: no `matches` arms and no `otherwise`

It is almost always a stub that was never filled in. Add the arms you meant:

    immut n: int = 1;
    compare n {
        matches 1:
            printline("one");
        done
        otherwise:
            printline("other");
    }

If you are only sketching and want the program to keep compiling cleanly, delete the empty `compare` until you have the arms.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E154` (or `[lints] E154 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

Comparing a bool with `true` or `false` is redundant

A condition that is already a `bool` doesn't need to be compared with a `bool` literal. The comparison just hands back the value you started with:

    immut ready: bool = true;
    if ready == true {     // WARNING: same as `if ready`
        printline("go");
    }

`ready == true` is exactly `ready`; `ready == false` and `ready != true` are exactly `!ready`; `ready != false` is `ready` again. Drop the comparison:

    immut ready: bool = true;
    if ready {
        printline("go");
    }

Turf only warns here when the comparison is one you wrote. A `!ready` you wrote is never flagged.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E155` (or `[lints] E155 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

A variable is assigned to itself

Assigning a variable to itself changes nothing:

    mut total: int = 3;
    total = total;         // WARNING: does nothing

It is nearly always a slip. Either a leftover from an edit, or the right-hand side was meant to be something else, like `total = total + 1;` or `total = other;`. Write the assignment you meant:

    mut total: int = 3;
    total = total + 1;

If the line is only a leftover, delete it.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E156` (or `[lints] E156 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

This loop looks like it never ends

A `while true` (or `do { } while true`) whose body has no `break`, no `continue` and no `return` has no way out, so the loop runs for ever:

    mut n: int = 0;
    while true {           // WARNING: nothing in the body can leave this loop
        n += 1;
    }

If you meant the loop to stop, give it an exit:

    mut n: int = 0;
    while true {
        n += 1;
        if n >= 10 {
            break;
        }
    }

Or put the stopping condition into the loop header and drop the `true`: `while n < 10 { ... }`.

This is a check on the shape of the loop, so it can fire on a loop that is meant to run for ever: a server's main loop, or a program that ends some other way, such as by calling `sys::exit`. When the loop is intentional, allowing the warning is the right tool, ideally with a comment saying why.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E157` (or `[lints] E157 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

This function has a lot of branches

Turf counts the independent paths through each function (its cyclomatic complexity: roughly one for the function, plus one for each extra path an `if`, a loop or a `compare` arm opens up). A function above 15 gets this warning:

    fn classify(n: int): int {
        if n == 1 { return 10; }
        if n == 2 { return 20; }
        if n == 3 { return 30; }
        if n == 4 { return 40; }
        if n == 5 { return 50; }
        if n == 6 { return 60; }
        if n == 7 { return 70; }
        if n == 8 { return 80; }
        if n == 9 { return 90; }
        if n == 10 { return 100; }
        if n == 11 { return 110; }
        if n == 12 { return 120; }
        if n == 13 { return 130; }
        if n == 14 { return 140; }
        if n == 15 { return 150; }
        return 0;           // WARNING: complexity 16, threshold 15
    }

A function with that many paths is hard to test and hard to read, since every path is a case you have to keep in your head. Usually the fix is to turn a long chain of `if`s into a `compare`, or to split the function into smaller helpers that each do one job:

    fn classify(n: int): int {
        compare n {
            matches 1: return 10; done
            matches 2: return 20; done
            matches 3: return 30; done
            otherwise: return 0;
        }
    }

The threshold is a style rule, not a correctness rule. Some functions are branchy for good reason (a parser, a big state switch), and splitting them would make them harder to follow. When that is the case, allowing the warning is the right tool.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E158` (or `[lints] E158 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

This function contains a loop it can never leave

Turf maps out every path through a function and looks for a loop whose paths all lead back into the loop. If nothing inside it can reach the code after it (no `break`, no `return`, no condition that can turn false), the function can never finish:

    fn spin(): int {            // WARNING: contains a loop with no exit path
        mut n: int = 0;
        while true {
            if n > 5 {
                continue;       // goes round again, never out
            }
            n += 1;
        }
        return n;
    }

Here `n` stops growing at 6 and the loop just keeps going round. The fix is to give the loop a real way out:

    fn spin(): int {
        mut n: int = 0;
        while true {
            if n > 5 {
                break;
            }
            n += 1;
        }
        return n;
    }

This warning belongs to the whole function, and it is found by looking at the control flow, so it also catches loops the simpler E157 check misses (a `continue` is enough to hide a loop from E157, not from this one). The two are often reported together, along with E074 for the code after the loop.

A loop that never ends can be exactly what you want, such as the main loop of a server, or a program that ends through `sys::exit`. In that case allowing the warning is the right tool.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E159` (or `[lints] E159 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

A value is stored and then thrown away

A variable is assigned a value that is replaced, or that the function returns without ever reading:

    fn main(): int {
        mut total: int;
        total = 10;         // WARNING: overwritten before anything reads it
        total = 20;
        return total - 20;
    }

Turf follows each variable through the function and notices that nothing reads the `10`. The store does no work, and it usually means one of two things: a line that should have been deleted, or a line that was meant to feed into the next one. If the second value was supposed to build on the first, say so:

    fn main(): int {
        mut total: int;
        total = 10;
        total += 10;        // reads the 10
        return total - 20;
    }

A store that does nothing today can still be harmless (a value kept for a debugger, or code you are about to use). If that is the case, a name starting with `_` is skipped, and allowing the warning covers the rest.

A variable that is declared but never read at all gets E163 instead, and a declaration's own initial value that is never read gets E161.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E160` (or `[lints] E160 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

The starting value of this variable is never used

A variable is declared with an initial value, but every path assigns it again before anything reads it, so the starting value is never seen:

    fn main(): int {
        mut result: int = 0;    // WARNING: assigned again before it is read
        result = 5;
        return result - 5;
    }

The `= 0` is dead weight, and it can hide a mistake: if you forget the later assignment on one path, the `0` quietly takes over. Declare the variable without a value and let Turf check that every read comes after an assignment:

    fn main(): int {
        mut result: int;
        result = 5;
        return result - 5;
    }

If a path can reach a read before the assignment, Turf rejects it (see E008), which is the point.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E161` (or `[lints] E161 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

This `for` range moves away from its end

A `for` over a numeric range counts up by default. If the start is already past the end, or the `step` goes the wrong way, the loop variable never gets to the end:

    mut n: int = 0;
    for i in 5..1 {        // WARNING: 5..1 counts down, but the step counts up
        n += i;
    }

`5..1` is a range that wants to go down, and nothing in the loop goes down. Without a `break` or `return` it would run for ever. The fix is a step that matches the direction:

    mut n: int = 0;
    for i in 5..1 step i-- {
        n += i;
    }

The same goes the other way: `for i in 1..5 step i--` counts down from a start that is below the end.

Turf only judges this when both bounds are plain integer constants and the step is the default, `i++`, `i--`, `i += k` or `i -= k`. Anything it cannot work out at compile time is left alone. The body may still leave the loop, so this is a warning and not an error.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E162` (or `[lints] E162 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

This variable is never used

A variable is declared, but nothing in the function ever reads it:

    fn main(): int {
        immut unused: int = 42;    // WARNING: declared but never used
        return 0;
    }

Unused variables are usually leftovers from an edit, or a sign that you forgot to use the value. Delete the declaration, or use the value. If the variable is unused on purpose (a placeholder, or a value you only want to name), start its name with an underscore. Turf treats `_name` as deliberately unused:

    fn main(): int {
        immut _unused: int = 42;
        return 0;
    }

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E163` (or `[lints] E163 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

The loop variable of this `for` never changes

In `for i in 0..10 step ... { }`, the `step` is what moves `i` along. If neither the `step` nor the body ever changes `i`, it stays at its start value, and the loop's fate is sealed before it starts:

    mut count: int = 0;
    mut j: int = 0;
    for i in 0..10 step j += 10 {   // WARNING: The loop variable `i` is never changed by this loop's `step` or its body
        count += 1;
        if count > 3 {
            break;
        }
    }

`step j += 10` moves `j`, not `i`. `i` is 0 for ever, `0 < 10` stays true for ever, and without the `break` the loop never ends. (If the range is empty from the start, the body never runs at all, and the warning says so.)

The fix is a step that moves the loop variable:

    mut count: int = 0;
    for i in 0..10 step i += 10 {
        count += 1;
    }

If a different variable is really what decides when the loop ends, use `while`, which says so directly.

Turf stays quiet when it cannot be sure: if the address of `i` is taken anywhere in the loop, something could be changing it through a pointer.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E164` (or `[lints] E164 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

This branch of an `if` is empty

An `if`, `elseif` or `else` whose block holds no statements does nothing:

    immut n: int = 3;
    if n > 2 {
    } else {                    // the 'then' branch is empty -- WARNING
        printline("small");
    }

The usual fix is to flip the condition so the work lives in the `if` and the empty branch goes away:

    immut n: int = 3;
    if n <= 2 {
        printline("small");
    }

If you simply haven't written the code yet, put a comment such as `// TODO` in the block. Turf counts a block holding only comments as empty too, so that does not silence the warning, but it does tell the next reader the branch is deliberate. An empty `else` can simply be deleted.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E165` (or `[lints] E165 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

A value is compared with itself

Comparing a variable with itself gives an answer that never changes:

    immut a: int = 3;
    if a == a {              // WARNING: always true
        printline("same");
    }

`a == a`, `a <= a` and `a >= a` are always true; `a != a`, `a < a` and `a > a` are always false. (For a `double`, `x != x` is the classic NaN check, and Turf's warning says "unless the value can be NaN" there.) It is nearly always a typo for a comparison with a different variable:

    immut a: int = 3;
    immut b: int = 3;
    if a == b {
        printline("same");
    }

If the self-comparison really is intentional, such as an `x != x` NaN test, allowing the warning is the right tool.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E166` (or `[lints] E166 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

Dividing by a literal zero

`/` or `%` with a literal `0` on the right is a division by zero, and it will crash the program when it runs:

    fn main(): int {
        immut n: int = 10;
        return n / 0;        // WARNING: division by zero
    }

Turf catches this when it can see the zero in your source. The right-hand side should be the real divisor. If it can legitimately be zero at run time, check for that first:

    fn main(): int {
        immut n: int = 10;
        immut d: int = 2;
        if d == 0 {
            return 1;
        }
        return n / d - 5;
    }

This warning only looks at literal `0` and `0.0`. A divisor that turns out to be zero only at run time is not caught, so a check like the one above is still the right habit.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E167` (or `[lints] E167 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

Both sides of this `if` do the same thing

When the `if` body and the `else` body are the same, the condition doesn't matter:

    immut n: int = 3;
    if n > 2 {
        printline("big");
    } else {
        printline("big");        // WARNING: Both branches of this `if` do the same thing.
    }

Either the branches really are the same, in which case the `if` can be dropped and the body kept, or one of them was copied and never edited. Change the one you forgot:

    immut n: int = 3;
    if n > 2 {
        printline("big");
    } else {
        printline("small");
    }

Turf compares the two bodies statement by statement, so differences in spacing or comments do not count. Only a plain `if` / `else` is checked, not a longer `elseif` chain.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E168` (or `[lints] E168 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

This function returns `!T` but can never fail

A return type of `!int` promises that the function can fail with an error. Turf warns when a function declared that way never returns an error and nothing it calls can fail either:

    fn double_it(n: int): !int {     // WARNING: never actually returns an error
        return n * 2;
    }

Every caller has to handle a `!int` with `recover` or `try`, so a function that cannot fail makes all of them do needless work. The fix is to drop the `!`:

    fn double_it(n: int): int {
        return n * 2;
    }

If the function does fail somewhere, return an error value on that path:

    error MathError {
        Negative,
    }

    fn checked_double(n: int): !int {
        if n < 0 {
            return MathError::Negative;
        }
        return n * 2;
    }

Sometimes the `!` is deliberate: a function that will fail later, or one that must match a signature other code expects. In that case allowing the warning is the right tool.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E169` (or `[lints] E169 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

This pointer may already have been freed

The compiler follows each allocation through the function. When a pointer has been released on some paths that reach a use, but not on all of them, it can't tell whether the use is safe:

    @ownership(acquires)
    extern fn malloc(size: int): int*;

    @ownership(releases: p)
    extern fn free(p: int*): void;

    fn main(): int {
        mut flag: int = 1;
        unsafe {
            mut q: int* = malloc(sizeof(int) * 4);
            if flag == 1 {
                free(q);
            }
            q[0] = 1;         // WARNING: `q` may already have been freed
        }
        return 0;
    }

If `flag == 1`, `q[0] = 1` writes to freed memory. Inside `main`, use the pointer before it can be released, and release it once, at the end:

    unsafe {
        mut q: int* = malloc(sizeof(int) * 4);
        q[0] = 1;
        if flag == 1 {
            printline("releasing");
        }
        free(q);
    }

Turf cannot evaluate your conditions, so this is a possible bug and not a certain one. If a flag or condition makes the freed path impossible where you use the pointer, the code is correct and you can allow the warning. When the pointer is freed on EVERY path before the use, that is the error E018 instead.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E170` (or `[lints] E170 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

This pointer may already have been freed before this release

The compiler follows each allocation through the function. When a pointer has been released on some paths that reach another `free`, but not on all of them, that second release may be a double free:

    import mem;

    fn main(): int {
        mut flag: int = 1;
        unsafe {
            mut p: void* = mem::alloc(8);
            if flag == 1 {
                mem::free(p);
            }
            mem::free(p);     // WARNING: `p` may already have been freed
        }
        return 0;
    }

When `flag == 1` the memory is released twice, which corrupts the allocator. Release the pointer on exactly one path:

    import mem;

    fn main(): int {
        mut flag: int = 1;
        unsafe {
            mut p: void* = mem::alloc(8);
            if flag == 1 {
                printline("releasing");
            }
            mem::free(p);
        }
        return 0;
    }

Turf cannot evaluate your conditions, so this is a possible bug and not a certain one. If a flag makes the two releases mutually exclusive (a `freed` variable that you test before the second call, for instance), the code is correct and you can allow the warning. A pointer freed on EVERY path before the second release is the error E017 instead.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E171` (or `[lints] E171 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

You're calling a deprecated item

A function (or `extern fn`) marked `@deprecated("...")` warns at every call to it, showing the message its declaration wrote:

    @deprecated("use new_sort() instead")
    fn old_sort(l: List<int>): List<int> {
        return l;
    }

    fn main(): int {
        old_sort(List::default());   // WARNING: `old_sort` is deprecated: use new_sort() instead
        return 0;
    }

The message names what to use instead, so the fix is usually to call that instead:

    fn main(): int {
        new_sort(List::default());
        return 0;
    }

The item itself still works exactly as before -- this is advance notice, not a removal. Turf never removes a deprecated item on its own; that only happens across a major version (`docs/planning/ROADMAP_v3.0.0.md`).

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E172` (or `[lints] E172 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

You're calling a test

A function marked `@test` is run by `turf test`, one test at a time, and every build leaves it out of the program. So nothing else may call it, or use it as a function value:

    @test
    fn parses_a_port(): void {
        test::assert(parse_port("8080") == 8080, "8080 is a port");
    }

    fn main(): int {
        parses_a_port();   // ERROR: I cannot use the test `parses_a_port`
        return 0;
    }

If the test and the program need the same steps, move them into an ordinary function and call that from both:

    fn check_port(): bool {
        return parse_port("8080") == 8080;
    }

    @test
    fn parses_a_port(): void {
        test::assert(check_port(), "8080 is a port");
    }

    fn main(): int {
        if check_port() {
            return 0;
        }
        return 1;
    }

To run a test, use `turf test`.

A decimal number is the receiver of a call without parentheses

A literal can be the receiver of a method call or a UFCS call, like a variable of its type. A decimal number written that way has two dots in a row of characters, its own and the call's:

    fn half(x: double): double {
        return x / 2.0;
    }

    fn main(): int {
        printline(1.5.half());   // WARNING (E174 at `warn`): 0.75
        return 0;
    }

Which dot ends the number is something the reader has to work out. Parentheses say it:

    fn main(): int {
        printline((1.5).half());   // 0.75
        return 0;
    }

The compiler's `Fix:` writes the parentheses in. The program means the same either way. An integer receiver (`1.twice()`) has only the call's dot and is fine as it is; so is `-1.abs()`, which is `-(1.abs())`.

This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E174` (or `[lints] E174 = "warn"` in turf.toml). TurfLang 3.0 makes the unparenthesized form an error, and `turf migrate 3.0` adds the parentheses.

`static` is written after the parameter list

A static method -- one with no `self`, called as `Type::name(...)` -- is written with `static` in front of `fn`:

    struct Counter {
        n: int
    }

    pub static fn Counter.limit(): int {
        return 100;
    }

The older place, after the parameter list, still compiles to the same method:

    pub fn Counter.limit() static: int {   // WARNING (E175 at `warn`)
        return 100;
    }

The compiler's `Fix:` moves the word. Writing `static` in both places is an error, and so is `static pub fn` (the order is `pub static fn`).

This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E175` (or `[lints] E175 = "warn"` in turf.toml). TurfLang 3.0 removes the old place, and `turf migrate 3.0` moves every `static` for you.

A generic construction takes its type arguments from the context

A generic struct built with `::default()` or `::new(...)` can take its type arguments from where its value goes -- the declared type of the variable, the function's return type, the parameter it is passed to:

    import list::List;

    fn main(): int {
        mut l: List<int> = List::default();   // WARNING (E177 at `warn`): <int> comes from `List<int>` on the left
        l.push(1);
        return 0;
    }

Read on its own, `List::default()` does not say what it builds. Write the type arguments where it is built:

    import list::List;

    fn main(): int {
        mut l: List<int> = List<int>::default();
        l.push(1);
        return 0;
    }

The compiler's `Fix:` writes them in, exactly as it already resolved them, so the program does not change. A constructor whose own arguments fix every type argument (`Box::new(5)` for a `fn<T> Box.new(v: T)`) takes nothing from the context and does not warn.

This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E177` (or `[lints] E177 = "warn"` in turf.toml). TurfLang 3.0 makes it an error (B-17), and `turf migrate 3.0` writes every one in.

Nothing calls or names a private function

A function or method declared without `pub` can only be used inside its own file. When nothing there calls it, passes it as a value, or calls it through UFCS, it is dead code:

    fn old_helper(x: int): int {   // WARNING (E178 at `warn`)
        return x * 2;
    }

    fn main(): int {
        return 0;
    }

Remove it, or call it where it was meant to be used. If another module is meant to call it, mark it `pub`.

`main`, operators, `extern fn` declarations and `@test` functions are never reported, and nothing in the standard library is.

This warning starts at `allow`: turn it on with `turf build --warn E178` (or `[lints] E178 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

Nothing in the file uses an import

An `import` that nothing in its file uses -- no `module::name`, no imported name, no method of one of the module's types -- only slows the build and misleads the reader:

    import math;   // WARNING (E179 at `warn`)

    fn main(): int {
        printline(2);
        return 0;
    }

The compiler's `Fix:` removes the line. The prelude modules (`io`, `math`, `strings`, ...) are always available, so importing one of them again is never reported.

This warning starts at `allow`: turn it on with `turf build --warn E179` (or `[lints] E179 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

A `pub` function in a program's root file that nothing calls

`pub` makes a declaration visible to other modules, but nothing imports a program's root file (the one with `main`), so there `pub` changes nothing. A `pub` function there that nothing calls is dead code, like a private one (E178):

    pub fn report(): int {   // WARNING (E180 at `warn`)
        return 1;
    }

    fn main(): int {
        return 0;
    }

Remove it or call it. A library module's `pub` functions are its interface and are never reported.

This warning starts at `allow`: turn it on with `turf build --warn E180` (or `[lints] E180 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

An `int` becomes a `double` and may lose precision

A `double` holds every whole number exactly only up to 2^53 (9007199254740992). An `int` goes up to 2^63 - 1, so converting a large one to `double` -- with `to double`, or by storing it into, passing it as, or returning it as a `double` -- rounds away its last digits:

    fn main(): int {
        mut big: int = 9007199254740993;
        mut d: double = big to double;   // WARNING (E181, when turned on): 9007199254740992.0
        printline(d);
        return 0;
    }

The warning is not shown when the compiler can prove the value is within +-2^53, for instance a constant or a literal that converts exactly.

This check is opt-in: it starts at `allow` and stays a warning in every version. Turn it on with `turf build --warn E181` (or `[lints] E181 = "warn"` in turf.toml) for code where exact large integers matter.

Functions call each other on every path, so none can return

Two or more functions that call each other form a cycle. When every path through every one of them calls back into the cycle -- no function has a way to return without calling another -- the program recurses until the stack runs out, exactly as a single function with no base case does (E010):

    fn is_even(n: int): bool {
        return is_odd(n - 1);    // WARNING (E182 at `warn`): no path returns
    }

    fn is_odd(n: int): bool {
        return is_even(n - 1);
    }

Give one of them a path that returns without calling back:

    fn is_even(n: int): bool {
        if n == 0 {
            return true;
        }
        return is_odd(n - 1);
    }

    fn is_odd(n: int): bool {
        if n == 0 {
            return false;
        }
        return is_even(n - 1);
    }

A call through a function value or to an `extern fn` never closes a cycle, since the compiler cannot see where it leads. This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E182` (or `[lints] E182 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

A recursive function's base case can never run

A recursive function stops through its base case: a path that returns without calling itself. When the values that reach the base case rule it out -- an earlier guard already excluded them, a constant never matches, a loop counter never gets there -- the base case is dead code, and every call that runs calls the function again until the stack runs out:

    fn countdown(n: int): int {
        if n < 1 {
            return countdown(1);
        }
        if n == 0 {
            return 0;            // WARNING (E183 at `warn`): n is at least 1 here
        }
        return countdown(n - 1);
    }

`countdown(1)` calls `countdown(0)`, which the first test sends back to `countdown(1)`. Make the base case reachable, for example by testing it first:

    fn countdown(n: int): int {
        if n <= 0 {
            return 0;
        }
        return countdown(n - 1);
    }

The compiler reports this only when it can prove the values: from constants, from a range `for`'s bounds, and from conditions earlier on the path. A base case it cannot reason about is left alone. A function with no base case at all is E010. This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E183` (or `[lints] E183 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

A loop's test never changes

The test of this `while` or `do while` loop reads only variables that nothing inside the loop writes, and the test is the only way out of the loop. So the test gives the same answer every time: if the loop starts, it never ends.

    fn wait(n: int): int {
        mut s: int = 0;
        while n > 0 {            // WARNING (E184 at `warn`): nothing changes `n`
            s += 1;
        }
        return s;
    }

Change the variable inside the loop, or give the loop another way out:

    fn wait(n: int): int {
        mut s: int = 0;
        mut left: int = n;
        while left > 0 {
            s += 1;
            left -= 1;
        }
        return s;
    }

The compiler counts every way a variable can change: an assignment, `+=`, `++`, and anything that takes its address (`&n`) or captures it in a closure. A test that calls a function or reads a field is never reported, since the call may change what it reads. This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E184` (or `[lints] E184 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

Every way out of a loop is a test that is never true

The loop has ways out -- its own test, a `break` or `return` behind an `if` -- but the compiler proves from the values the loop computes that none of them is ever taken:

    fn spin(): int {
        mut i: int = 0;
        while i >= 0 {           // WARNING (E185 at `warn`): i is always 0..9
            i = (i + 1) % 10;
        }
        return i;
    }

`(i + 1) % 10` is never negative, so `i >= 0` is always true. Fix the test, or the computation it is meant to watch:

    fn spin(): int {
        mut i: int = 0;
        while i < 9 {
            i = (i + 1) % 10;
        }
        return i;
    }

The proof uses constants, a range `for`'s bounds, tests earlier on the path and what `%`, `&` and `>>` leave of a value. A test the compiler cannot reason about is never reported. This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E185` (or `[lints] E185 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

Every way out of a loop comes after a call that ends the program

Each way out of this loop that can be taken passes a call that never returns -- `sys::panic`, `sys::abort` or `sys::exit` -- so the code after the loop never runs:

    fn find(limit: int): int {
        mut j: int = 0;
        while true {             // WARNING (E186 at `warn`)
            j += 1;
            if j > limit {
                sys::panic("too far");
                break;           // never reached
            }
        }
        return j;
    }

If the loop is meant to stop, let it leave before the call:

    fn find(limit: int): int {
        mut j: int = 0;
        while j <= limit {
            j += 1;
        }
        return j;
    }

If ending the program is the intended way out, the `break` after the call is dead code: remove it and write the loop as `while true`. This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E186` (or `[lints] E186 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

Manual-heap memory that is never freed

Memory from `mem::alloc` (and every other function declared `@ownership(acquires)`) lives outside the garbage collector: it stays allocated until something calls `mem::free` on it. This allocation is kept in a variable that nothing frees and nothing passes on, or its result is thrown away, so the memory is lost for the rest of the program:

    import mem;

    fn scratch(): void {
        unsafe {
            mut buf: void* = mem::alloc(32);   // WARNING (E187 at `warn`)
            mem::zero(buf, 32);
        }
    }

Free it when you are done. `defer` right after the allocation frees it on every path out of the function:

    import mem;

    fn scratch(): void {
        unsafe {
            mut buf: void* = mem::alloc(32);
            defer mem::free(buf);
            mem::zero(buf, 32);
        }
    }

The compiler is conservative: memory that is stored in a field or an array, passed to a call that may keep it (a `mem::Pool`, `mem::root`), converted, or captured by a closure may be freed elsewhere, so it is never reported. Garbage-collected values are never reported. This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E187` (or `[lints] E187 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

A raw pointer that may be null is dereferenced

A raw pointer (`T*`, `ptr`) can be null, and nothing checks it at run time: reading a field, an index or a method through a null pointer crashes the program. This dereference is not proven safe, because the pointer was not tested against `null` on every path that reaches it and does not come from something that is never null:

    import mem;

    fn first(): int {
        unsafe {
            mut p: int* = mem::malloc(16) to int*;   // malloc may return null
            p[0] = 1;                                // WARNING (E188 at `warn`)
            return p[0];
        }
    }

Test the pointer first, or get it from something that is never null: `mem::alloc` (which stops the program instead of returning null), `&x`, or `self`:

    import mem;

    fn first(): int {
        unsafe {
            mut p: int* = mem::alloc(16) to int*;
            p[0] = 1;
            mut v: int = p[0];
            mem::free(p);
            return v;
        }
    }

Only a pointer held in a variable is checked; a pointer read from a field is not. This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E188` (or `[lints] E188 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

Freeing memory that was never allocated

`mem::free` gives back memory that an allocation (`mem::alloc` and the other `@ownership(acquires)` functions) handed out. This frees something that did not come from one: the address of a variable. That memory belongs to the function's frame, and freeing it corrupts the allocator:

    import mem;

    fn wrong(): int {
        mut x: int = 5;
        unsafe {
            mut p: void* = &x to void*;
            mem::free(p);                // WARNING (E189 at `warn`)
        }
        return x;
    }

A variable's memory is released when its function returns; there is nothing to free. Free only what an allocation returned:

    import mem;

    fn right(): int {
        unsafe {
            mut p: void* = mem::alloc(8);
            mem::free(p);
        }
        return 0;
    }

The compiler reports this only when every value the freed pointer could hold is such an address; a pointer that is ever given an allocation, a parameter or a call's result is never reported. This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E189` (or `[lints] E189 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

A write through a pointer into an `immut` array

A pointer made from an `immut` array (`&a to int*`) points at values declared never to change. Writing through it changes them anyway, behind the compiler's back, which may also have used the old values already:

    fn patch(): int {
        immut a: int[4] = [1, 2, 3, 4];
        unsafe {
            mut p: int* = &a to int*;
            p[0] = 9;                    // WARNING (E190 at `warn`)
        }
        return a[0];
    }

Declare the array `mut` if it is meant to change:

    fn patch(): int {
        mut a: int[4] = [1, 2, 3, 4];
        unsafe {
            mut p: int* = &a to int*;
            p[0] = 9;
        }
        return a[0];
    }

The compiler reports this only when every value the pointer could hold points into the same `immut` array. This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E190` (or `[lints] E190 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

A raw pointer indexed with no bound

Indexing a raw pointer (`p[i]`) is pointer arithmetic: nothing checks that `i` stays inside the memory `p` points to, unlike an array index. The compiler warns when it can see no bound on the index -- it is not proven non-negative, or nothing limits it from above:

    import mem;

    fn fill(n: int): int {
        unsafe {
            mut p: int* = mem::alloc(80) to int*;
            for i in 0..n {
                p[i] = i;                // WARNING (E191 at `warn`): n may exceed 10
            }
            mem::free(p);
        }
        return 0;
    }

Make the bound visible, so the index provably stays inside the allocation:

    import mem;

    fn fill(n: int): int {
        unsafe {
            mut p: int* = mem::alloc(80) to int*;
            for i in 0..10 {
                p[i] = i;
            }
            mem::free(p);
        }
        return n;
    }

The bound comes from constants, a range `for`, tests on the path and `%`/`&` (the same ranges that remove overflow checks). This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E191` (or `[lints] E191 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

An integer converted to a pointer

`x to T*` with an integer `x` makes a pointer to whatever happens to be at that address. The compiler cannot know what is there, or whether anything is:

    fn device(): int {
        unsafe {
            mut port: int* = 4096 to int*;   // WARNING (E192 at `warn`)
        }
        return 0;
    }

Get a pointer from what it points to -- `&x`, an allocation -- or write `null` for "no address":

    fn device(): int {
        mut value: int = 0;
        unsafe {
            mut port: int* = &value to int*;
            mut none: int* = null;
        }
        return value;
    }

This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E192` (or `[lints] E192 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

An `unsafe` block that nothing needs

`unsafe { }` marks the code that needs care: raw-pointer declarations, casts and dereferences. This block holds none of them, so it marks nothing and makes the real unsafe code harder to find:

    fn bump(): int {
        mut y: int = 1;
        unsafe {                     // WARNING (E193 at `warn`)
            y += 1;
        }
        return y;
    }

Remove the `unsafe { }` and keep its statements:

    fn bump(): int {
        mut y: int = 1;
        y += 1;
        return y;
    }

A block counts as needed when anything inside it, including a nested block, uses what `unsafe` allows. A top-level `unsafe { }` around a declaration is not checked. This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E193` (or `[lints] E193 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

A very large `unsafe` block

This `unsafe { }` block holds more than 40 statements, counting those of the blocks inside it. The few operations that need care are hard to find among the rest, and every statement inside is exempt from the raw-pointer checks:

    fn work(): int {
        mut t: int = 0;
        unsafe {                     // WARNING (E194 at `warn`)
            mut raw: int* = &t to int*;
            t += 1;
            // ... forty more statements that need no `unsafe`
        }
        return t;
    }

Keep only the raw-pointer operations inside `unsafe { }`, and move the rest out, or into a function:

    fn work(): int {
        mut t: int = 0;
        unsafe {
            mut raw: int* = &t to int*;
            raw[0] = 1;
        }
        t += 1;
        return t;
    }

This warning starts at `allow` in 2.3: turn it on with `turf build --warn E194` (or `[lints] E194 = "warn"` in turf.toml).

An empty loop body

A `while`, `do`, `for` or `for ... in` loop whose body holds no statements does nothing on each pass:

    mut arr: int[3] = [1, 2, 3];
    for v in arr {
    }                           // the body is empty -- WARNING

Write what the loop should do, or remove the loop. A `while` whose condition calls a function does its work in the test (`while advance(&n) { }`), so it does not warn.

If the loop is meant to do nothing, put a comment in the body saying why. A body holding a comment does not warn:

    for i in 0..3 {
        // counted, nothing else
    }

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E195` (or `[lints] E195 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

An empty function body

A function that returns nothing and whose body holds no statements does nothing when it is called:

    fn reset(): void {
    }                           // the body is empty -- WARNING

Write the function, or remove it and its calls. A `@test` function may be empty (an empty test passes by returning), and a function that returns a value is reported by E009 instead.

If the function is meant to do nothing, put a comment in the body saying why. A body holding a comment does not warn:

    fn reset(): void {
        // nothing to reset: the defaults are right
    }

A generic function warns once, however many types it is used with.

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E196` (or `[lints] E196 = "allow"` in turf.toml).

An empty `recover` block

`recover ... run { }` handles a failure. An empty block handles nothing: the error is dropped without a trace, which is what `recover` exists to prevent.

    import fs;

    fn main(): int {
        fs::mkdir("out") recover as err run {
        };                      // the error is dropped -- WARNING
        return 0;
    }

Handle the error in the block: report it, return it, or use a default:

    import fs;

    fn main(): int {
        fs::mkdir("out") recover as err run {
            printline("could not create out/");
        };
        return 0;
    }

If ignoring the error is right, put a comment in the block saying why. A block holding a comment does not warn:

        fs::mkdir("out") recover as err run {
            // the directory may exist already, which is fine
        };

This is a warning, not an error -- compilation still succeeds. To silence it for a whole build, set its lint level to allow: `turf build --allow E197` (or `[lints] E197 = "allow"` in turf.toml).
